      },
      {
        id: "m1-2",
        moduleId: "m1",
        number: 7,
        title: "CIA Triad (Confidentiality, Integrity, Availability)",
        type: "theory",
        week: 1,
        description:
          "The three foundational principles of information security — every security control you'll learn about maps to at least one of these three.",
        keyPoints: [
          "Confidentiality: data is accessible only to authorised individuals",
          "Integrity: data remains accurate and trustworthy (unmodified)",
          "Availability: data/services remain accessible to legitimate users when needed",
          "Example: encrypting a file protects Confidentiality; a hash verifies Integrity; a backup ensures Availability",
        ],
      },
      {
        id: "m1-3",
        moduleId: "m1",
        number: 8,
        title: "Types of Cybersecurity Domains (Offensive, Defensive, GRC)",
        type: "theory",
        week: 1,
        description:
          "Cybersecurity splits into domains with distinct goals — this course focuses on Offensive Security, but understanding Defensive and GRC gives you the full picture of how a security organisation operates.",
        keyPoints: [
          "Offensive Security: simulating attacks to find vulnerabilities before attackers do (pentesting, red teaming, bug bounty)",
          "Defensive Security: protecting systems/data, patching, and preventing unauthorised access (blue team, SOC)",
          "GRC (Governance, Risk, Compliance): policies/frameworks, risk assessment, and meeting legal standards (GDPR, ISO 27001, HIPAA)",
          "Other domains: Cloud Security, Application Security, Network Security, Incident Response, IAM",
        ],
      },
      {
        id: "m1-4",
        moduleId: "m1",
        number: 9,
        title: "Common Threats & Attack Types (Malware, Phishing, MITM, DDoS, Ransomware)",
        type: "theory",
        week: 1,
        description:
          "A survey of the attack categories you'll encounter throughout the programme, each of which gets its own deep-dive module later (web attacks in M9/M10, network attacks in M2/M7/M11).",
        keyPoints: [
          "Malware: software designed to damage/gain unauthorised access (viruses, worms, trojans, spyware)",
          "Phishing: deceptive messages tricking users into revealing credentials or installing malware",
          "MITM (Man-in-the-Middle): intercepting/modifying traffic between two parties, e.g. via ARP spoofing",
          "DDoS: overwhelming a service with traffic from many sources to make it unavailable",
          "Ransomware: encrypts victim data and demands payment for the decryption key",
