      },
      {
        id: "m1-5",
        moduleId: "m1",
        number: 10,
        title: "Key Terms: CVE, CWE, IOC, APT, Zero-Day, Threat Actor, TTP",
        type: "theory",
        week: 1,
        description:
          "Core vocabulary you'll see constantly in vulnerability reports, threat intel feeds, and pentest reports — get these definitions locked in early. Full definitions with real-world examples are in the Glossary.",
        keyPoints: [
          "CVE: unique public ID for a specific known vulnerability (e.g. CVE-2021-44228 = Log4Shell)",
          "CWE: a class/root-cause of weakness (e.g. CWE-79 = the root cause behind all XSS)",
          "IOC (Indicator of Compromise): evidence of a breach — suspicious IPs, hashes, registry keys",
          "APT (Advanced Persistent Threat): a sophisticated, long-term, often state-sponsored attack campaign",
          "Zero-Day: a vulnerability unknown to the vendor with no patch available",
          "TTPs: Tactics (goals), Techniques (how), Procedures (specific implementation) describing attacker behaviour",
